1700:
** ἐγώ**,
I: _pronoun_ of the first person:—_Epic dialect_ mostly **ἐγών** before vowels (so in _Doric dialect_, before consonants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 85, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 748, 754), rarely in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 932 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱών** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 51.4}}:— strengthened **ἔγωγε**, **I at least, for my part, indeed, for myself** (more frequently in _Attic dialect_ than in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἐγώνγα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 736, “Lys.” 986, _dative_ ἐμίνγα “IG” 22.1126.7 (Amphict. Delph.)}}: _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱώνγα** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 21}}; **ἱώνει** {{Refs|Ead. 10}}; **ἰώγα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 898}}: _Laconian dialect_ and _Tarentum dialect_ **ἐγώνη**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 255.29.}}
**II** oblique cases from a different root, _genitive_ ἐμοῦ, _enclitic_ μο; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἐμέο, ἐμεῦ, μευ, also ἐμέθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.525, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 177 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμεθεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.7; ἐμεῖο “IG” 3.1337; μεθέν 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 20}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμέος, ἐμεῦς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 144}}; _Boeotian dialect_ ἐμοῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 37; also ἐμῶς, ἐμίο, ἐμίω, ἐμίως 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.17:}} — _dative_ ἐμοί, _enclitic_ μοι (which may be compared with Sanskrit _genitive_ **me** in κλῦθί μοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.115, +others); ὅ μοι πόσις “Schwyzer” 683 (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 99, “AJA” 29.461 (Rhodian, 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 733, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.30}}; _Tarentum dialect_ ἐμίνη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13: _accusative_ ἐμέ}}, _enclitic_ μ; {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria μι “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 59,60 H.}}
**III** dual, _nominative_ and _accusative_, **νῶι**, **we two**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.34, etc.}}; _accusative_ νῶιν Zenod.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.377}}; _Attic dialect_ νώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278b (also 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.219, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.475); νῶε 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 39, 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 5: _genitive_, _dative_ νῶι; νῷν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 3}}; νῶι _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 773}}; νῶιν, ={ἡμῖν}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.213, etc.}}
**IV** _plural_, _nominative_ ἡμεῖς (ἡμέες _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.6, +others, rejected by 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.1)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.303, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.144}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμές {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 65, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168:— _genitive_ ἡμῶν (also ἥμων 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.23)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, etc.; ἡμείων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.170, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.46}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἀμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 88, “Milet.” 3 No.152.29}}; ἄμμων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]74, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 66}}; ἁμῶν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.158}}; Cretan dialect, _Boeotian dialect_ ἁμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 528.5, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.21:— _dative_ ἡμῖν, in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus also ἡμίν}} (ῐ) (or ἧμιν Aristarch.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.214, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3)}}; also rarely in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 37, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 386 (uncertain)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμῐν, ἄμμῐ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.384, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 80, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.155, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 156 (Lyric poetry), “Milet.” 3 No.152; ἄμμεσιν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 100}}; _Doric dialect_ also ἁμίν or ἇμιν, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 77, 78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 347 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1081}}; with ῑ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 821, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.145:— _accusative_ ἡμᾶς (also ἧμᾰς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.372)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.211, “SIG” 273.25 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; ἥμεας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.294 (cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.140)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.59, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 115}}, Theocr.8.25; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 759 codices, “Lys.” 95.}}—On these dialectic varieties, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 50}} _ff._ (Cf. Sanskrit _ahám_ (ἐγών), _accusative_ _plural_ **asmā´n**; for νώ cf. Sanskrit _nau_):—frequently in answers, as an affirmative, especially in form ἔγωγε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149b, etc.}}; οὗτος ἐ. here am {{Refs 5th c.BC+|I, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; ὅδ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 138 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with _Article_, τὸν ἐμέ **myself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Sph.” 239b (but ὁ ἐ.}} **the Self, the Ego**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 444)}}; τίς ὢν οὗτος ὁ ἐγὼ τυγχάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1119a; τί ἐστι φίλο; ἄλλος ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.166 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}; τί τοῦτ᾽ ἐμο; ἡμῖν τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ; Latin _quid mea hoc refert_ ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 498, etc.}}; ἐγ; in a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1336, +others}}; ἡμεῖς **the self**, ἔνθα δὴ ἡμεῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.1.7.}}

1702:
** ἐμπαίζω**,
_future_ **-ξομαι**{{LXX|LXX.Hab.1.10:}}_perfect_ ἐμπέπαιχα{{LXX|LXX.Num.22.29}}:— **mock at, mock**, τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134; τινά “PCair.Preis.” 3.10 (4th c.AD): _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 799}}:—_passive_, ψυχὴ ὑπό.. σωμάτων καὶ πραγμάτων ἐμπαιζομένη {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.568, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Trag.” 333.}}
**2** euphemistic in mal. _participle_, {{LXX|LXX.Josh.19.25.}}
**3** _passive_, **to be deluded,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.2.16, “Anthologia Graeca” 10.56.2 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 16.14}}; **to be defrauded**, of the revenues, {{Refs|“Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.34.2.}}
**II** **sport in** or **on**, ὡς νεβρὸς χλοεραῖς ἐ. λείμακος ἡδοναῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 866 (Lyric poetry)}}; τοῖς χοροῖσιν ἐ. **to sport in** the dance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 975; τῷ γυμνασίῳ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 5.}}

1704:
** ἐμπερι-πᾰτέω**,
**walk about in**, [ἐμβάταις] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 6; μέσοις τοῖς ἁγίοις 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.3.10: _metaphorically_, ταῖς διανοίαις 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.643, compare 274}}; ἐ. ἐν ὑμῖν **tarry among** you, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Lev.26.12, cf. NT.2Cor.6.16}}: _absolutely_, **walk about**, ἅμα τῷ συμποσίῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 13: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἐ. διαύλους τινάς}} **walk** several times to and fro, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.6.}}
**II** **walk about upon**, τὴν ὑπ᾽ οὐρανόν (i.e. γῆν) {{LXX|LXX.Job.1.7, +others}}; **trample on,** {{Refs|PHolm. 18.30}}: _metaphorically_, **insult**, τινί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.57a.}}

1705:
** ἐμπίμπλημι**,
_Ionic dialect2nd pers._ _singular_ _present_ ἐμπιπλεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.14}}_participle_ -πιπλῶν{{Refs|[prev. work] 12}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐμπιπλέει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.39 (with variants -πιπλεῖ, -πιπλᾷ)}}: _1st pers._ _singular_ _imperfect_ ἐνεπίμπλων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 68.31}}: _future_ **-πλήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 875c:}}_aorist_ ἐνέπλησα, _Epic dialect_ _subjunctive_ ἐνιπλήσῃς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.117:}}_perfect_ ἐμπέπληκα (see. below):—**fill quite full**, ἐν ὦν ἔπλησαν τοῦ νεκροῦ τὴν κοιλίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.87}}; τὸ πεδίον, τὴν ὁδόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.20, 2.4.11.}}
**2** with _genitive_, **fill full of** a thing, ἐμπίπληθι ῥέεθρα ὕδατος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.311, etc.; δέπας ὕδατος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.209; [ἵππον] ἀνδρῶν ἐμπλήσας 8.495; μὴ.. θυμὸν ἐνιπλήσῃς ὀδυνάων 19.117; ἐ. [τὰ θυλάκια] τῆς ψάμμου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.105, compare 4.72, 5.114}}; τοὺς κοφίνους.. ἐμπίμπλη (_imperative_) πτερῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1310; ἐ. ἵππων τὸν ἱππόδρομον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 3.10: _metaphorically_, τὴν ψυχὴν ἔρωτος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 255d; τινὰ ἐλπίδων κενῶν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.171.}}
**3** **fill** a hungry man **with food**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.503.}}
**3.b** _metaphorically_, ἐ. τινὰ μύθων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 769; τοῦ πολεμεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.63; ἐκκεκώφωκε τὰ ὦτα καὶ ἐμπέπληκε Δύσιδος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204c; ἐρώτων.. ἐμπίμπλησιν ἡμᾶς [prev. author] “Phd.” 66c; ἐμπιμπλὰς ἁπάντων τὴν γνώμην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.8.}}
**4** **satiate**, τὴν ἀναιδῆ γνώμην αὑτοῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.91; ἵμερον 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.429; ἕως νυκτὸς ἀλλήλους 3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 2.38.}}
**5** **fulfil, accomplish**, τὴν αὑτοῦ μοῖραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 959c.}}
**II** _middle_ (with _aorist_ _passive_), ἐμπίμπλαμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 925; ἐμπιμπλάμενος 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 142, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 80, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 117G.}}: _imperfect_ ἐνεπιμπλάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.46, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.230, etc.}}: later _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐνεπιμπλῶντο {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 34 / 5.2.29}}:—**fill for oneself** or **what is one's own**, ἐμπλήσατο νηδύν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.296}}; μένεος ἐμπλήσατο θυμόν he **filled his** heart **with** rage, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.312; θαλέων ἐμπλησάμενος κῆρ [prev. work] 504}}; τὸ ἄγγος τοῦ ὕδατος ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.12.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **eat oneself full, eat one's fill**, ἐνιπλησθῆναι ἀνώγει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.221, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.117, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 911, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.6, etc.: _metaphorically_, ἐπειδὴ τάχιστα ἐνέπληντο (ἐνεπέπληντο codices) 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.6.}}
**III** _passive_, _aorist 1_ ἐνεπλήσθην (see. below): _aorist 2_ ἐνεπλήμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 911, 1304, probably in 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.6}}; _optative_ ἐμπλῄμην (see. below): _pluperfect_ ἐνεπεπλήμην _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator [same place], late ἐμπέπληστο 2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 18.7; ἐνέπλησθεν δέ οἱ.. αἵματος ὀφθαλμοί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.348; δακρύων τὰ ὄμματα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.10; ἔμπληντο βροτῶν ἀγοραί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.16; πόλις δ᾽ ἔμπλητο ἀλέντων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.607; ἐνέπλητο πολλῶν κἀγαθῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1304}}; φακῆς ἐμπλήμενος{{Refs|[prev. work] 984, compare “Ec.” 56: _metaphorically_, υἷος ἐνιπλησθῆναι.. ὀφθαλμοῖσιν}} **to take my fill of** my son with my eyes, i.e. **to sate myself with** looking on him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.452; ὀργῆς καὶ μένους ἐμπλήμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 424; πλεονεξίας ἐμπίμπλασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 121b.}}
**III.2** with _dative_, ἀμπελίνῳ καρπῷ ἐ. **to be filled with**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.212; ἐμπιπλάμενοι πυριάτῃ 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 142; ἐμπίπλαται.. αἵματι ὁ βωμός 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.16.10.}}
**III.3** with _participle_, μισῶν οὔποτ᾽ ἐμπλησθήσομαι γυναῖκας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 664, compare “Ion” 925; βάλλων.. οὐκ ἂν ἐμπλῄμην 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 236; οὐκ ἐνεπίμπλασο ὑπισχνούμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.46}}; ἔμπλησο λέγων speak thy **fill**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 603.}} —The two last constructions are post-Homeric. (Freq. written -πίπλ-, but the evidence of the best codices of _Attic dialect_ writers is in favour of -πίμπλ-.)

1706:
** ἐμπίπτω**,
_future_ -πεσοῦμαι: _aorist_ ἐνέπεσον, _Epic dialect_ ἔμπεσον (see. below): Lyric poetry _aorist_ ἔμπετες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.81}}:—**fall in** or **on**, with _dative_, τρύφος ἔμπεσε πόντῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.508; ὁ δ᾽ ὕπτιος ἔμπεσε πέτρῃ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.108; ἐν δ᾽ ἔπεσ᾽ ὠκεανῷ, of the Sun, 8.485}}; πῦρ ἔμπεσε νηυσίν fire **fell upon** them,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|16.113; αὐχένι.. ἔμπεσεν ἰός 15.451, compare 624; with ἐν, ὡς δ᾽ ὅτε πῦρ.. ἐν ἀξύλῳ ἐμπέσῃ ὕλῃ 11.155; κεραυνοὶ αὐτοῖσι ἐνέπιπτον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.37}}; ἐμπέσοι γέ σοι (i.e. ὁ πύργος) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 180, etc.: _absolutely_, ῥύμῃ ἐ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34: with _genitive_, ὠκεανοῖο 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 635.}}
**b** Geometry texts, **meet**, of a line **meeting** another, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1 “Post.” 5, etc.}}; **to be placed**, ἐὰν εἰς τὸν κύκλον εὐθεῖα ἐμπέσῃ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.9; ἡ ἐμπεσοῦσα [prev. passage]}}
**c** of a dislocated limb, **fall into place**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 8.}}
**2** **fall upon, attack**, ἐν δ᾽ ἔπεσον προμάχοις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.526, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.81; στρατῷ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 127; τοῖς πολεμίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 8.25, etc.}}; ἐμπεσόντες **having fallen on** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.146, compare 7.16.ά}}: _metaphorically_, **insult**, ἄλλοισι δ᾽ ἐμπίπτων γελᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.68}}; so,
**3** of evils, diseases, etc., **fall on** one, **attack**, κακὸν ἔμπεσε οἴκῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.45; λὺγξ τοῖς πλέοσιν ἐνέπιπτε κενή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49; νόσημα ἐμπέπτωκεν εἰς τὴν Ἑλλάδα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.259; πρὶν ἐμπεσεῖν σπαραγμόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1253}}; ὕπνος ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 45e: of passions, of frames of mind, χόλος, δέος ἔμπεσε θυμῷ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.436, 17.625; ἔρως μή τις ἐμπίπτῃ στρατῷ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 341; Ἔρως, ὃς ἐν κτήμασι πίπτεις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 782 (Lyric poetry); ἐμοὶ.. οἶκτος [prev. author] “Ph.” 965; τοῖς Ἀθηναίοις ἐνέπεσέ τι γέλωτος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28; μὴ λύσσα τις ἡμῖν ἐμπεπτώκοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.26; ἔλεος ἐμπέπτωκέ τίς μοι 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 9.1}}; ἐ. εἰς.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.43, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 443, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.48 codices, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.18, etc.: rarely with _accusative_, οὐδείς ποτ᾽ αὐτοὺς.. ἂν ἐμπέσοι ζῆλος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 942; ἐμπέπτωκ᾽ ἔρως.. Ἑλλάδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 808.}}
**3.b** of words, καί μοι ἔπος ἔμπεσε θυμῷ **came into** my mind, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.266}}; λόγος ἐμπέπτωκεν ἀρτίως ἐμοί **came to** my ears, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1150}}; κἂν περὶ ἀνδρῶν γ᾽ ἐμπέσῃ λόγος τις a report **arose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 858, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 354b, “Lg.” 799d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 2.2}}; so τόποι ἐμπίπτοντες **available, suitable** topics, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “προγυμνάσματα” 7, etc., compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.179.}}
**4** **light** or **fall upon**, πρὶν ἁλίῳ γυῖον ἐμπεσεῖν before his body **was exposed** to the sun, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.73; [θηρία] ἐμπίπτοντα ταῖς ὄψεσι 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.9.5}}; also εἰς τὴν ὄψιν, εἰς τὴν αἴσθησιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 67d, “R.” 524d.}}
**4.b** **fall into**, ἐ. ἐν ἀπορίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 293a; ἐπὶ συμφορήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.88; more frequently ἐ. εἰς.., ἐ. εἰς ἄτας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 216 (Lyric poetry); εἰς βάρβαρα φάσγανα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 864; εἰς ἐνέδραν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.14; εἰς ἔρωτα 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 235.3; εἰς νόσον 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.20; εἰς ὑποψίας [prev. author] 2.2.3; εἰς φαῦλον σκέμμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 435c; εἴς τινα βυθὸν φλυαρίας [prev. author] “Prm.” 130d; εἰς πράγματα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.292}}; ἐ. εἰς τὰ πεπραγμένα, in speaking, **come upon** the exploits, {{Refs|[prev. work]211; εἰς λόγους [prev. work]42, compare 59.}}
**5** τῷ ἀκοντίῳ ἐ. τῷ ὤμῳ **throw oneself on** the javelin with one's shoulder, i.e. to give all one's force to the throw, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 20.}}
**6** **break in, burst in**, στέγῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1262; πύλαις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1146; εἰς τὴν θύραν κριηδόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 309}}; **intrude**, εἰς τὸ ἀρχεῖον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1270b9: _absolutely_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1350}}; ἐμπεσών **violently, rashly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81.}}
**7** εἴς τι **fall within** the province of, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 205d; εἰς τὰς εἰρημένας αἰτίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 986a15, compare “Rh.” 1401b29, “Ph.” 196b9; εἰς ἄλλο πρόβλημα [prev. author] “Pol.” 1268b25.}}
**7.b** of income, εἰς τὸν λόγον τινὸς ἐ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PLille” 16.5 (3rd c.BC),compare “POxy.” 494.21 (2nd c.AD).}}
**7.c** of suits, **come before**, εἰς δικαστῶν πλῆθος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1300b35, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 18.}}
**8** ἐ. εἰς δεσμωτήριον **to be thrown** into prison, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.9, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.60}}(_absolutely_, **get into prison**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 28); εἰς ζήτρειον 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 19 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; so ἐ. εἰς τὸν Τάρταρον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114a: Comedy texts, εἰς τὸν οὐρανόν “Comica Adespota” 9D.}}
**9** of circumstances, **happen, occur**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 7.8.4.}}
**10** **desert**, πρός τινα {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.25.11.}}

1707:
** ἐμπλέκ-ω**,
_Epic dialect_ **ἐνιπλέκω**, _perfect_ ἐμπέπλεχα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὀστέων φύσιος” 17, ἐμπέπλεκα 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Iambi” 1.352}}, variant in Hp.[same place]: _future_ _passive_ ἐμπλᾰκήσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Pro.28.18}}:—**plait** or **weave in, entwine**, χεῖρα ἐ. **entwine** one's hand **in** another's clothes, so as to hold him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 262; εἰς ἀρκυστάταν μηχανὰν ἐμπλέκειν παῖδα [prev. work] 1421 (Lyric poetry)}}; τῇ καλλίστῃ τέχνῃ τοὔνομα ἐ. **connect** the name **with**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 244c; ποιηταὶ τοιαῦτα ἐμπλέκοντες καὶ συγκυκῶντες [prev. author] “Lg.” 669d; ἐ. τὴν ἡδονὴν εἰς τὴν εὐδαιμονίαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1153b15}}; ποίῃ ἐνιπλέξω σε (i.e. ἀοιδῇ); {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 29; ἐ. τινὰ εἰς φιλίαν τινός 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 27.7.11}}:—_passive_, **to be entangled in** a thing, πλεκταῖσιν αἰώραισιν ἐμπεπλεγμένην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1264; ἡνίαισιν ἐμπλακείς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1236; ἐν δεσμοῖσιν ἐμπεπλεγμένη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1032; εἰς δίκτυον ἄτης ἐμπλεχθήσεσθε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1079}}: _metaphorically_, **to be involved**, ἐν πόνοις, ἐν κακοῖς ἐμπλακῆναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 814e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.112; εἰς ἀσχολίας βαθυτέρας τῶν ἐγκυκλίων 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.35U.; εἰς τὰ κατὰ τὴν Σικελίαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.17.3}}; **form a connexion with**, ἔθνει {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 24.6.1; γυναικὶ ἐμπλακείς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.2; εἴς τινα Vett. Val. 118.4}}; of troops, **to be incorporated with** hoplites, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 6.1; but also ἐμπλεκέντες τινί}}**having had a scuffle with..,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 39.17 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **weave by subtle art**, ἐ. αἰνίγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 610; ἐ. πλοκάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 936.}}

1708:
** ἐμ-πλοκή**, ἡ,
**braiding**, κόμης {{NT+4th c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.3.7, compare 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus Dam. p.2 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, NT.1Pet.3.3.}}
**2** **scuffle,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PRyl. 124.28 (1st c.AD), 150.12 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** **interweaving**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 1420 (uncertain)}}; **entanglement**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.916d (_plural_)}}; of the **matted** roots of trees, {{Refs|Philo Byzantius Paradoxographus “de Septem Miraculis” 1.5 (_plural_)}}; τόποις ἐμπλοκὰς ἔχειν, of districts, **to run into one another**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.4.12.}}
**III** in Mathematics texts, κατ᾽ ἐμπλοκήν, ={ἐμπλέγδην}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.124P., +others}}

1709:
** ἐμπνέω**,
_poetry_ **ἐμ-πνείω**, _future_ **-πνεύσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 555}}; later **-πνεύσω** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Aeneas Gazaeus Rhetor “Epistulae” 11}}:—**blow** or **breathe upon**, with _dative_, πόντῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 508}}; ἐμπνείοντε μεταφρένῳ, of horses so close behind as **to breathe upon** one's back, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.502}}; of a lover, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; κατ᾽ οὖρον, ὥσπερ ἱστίοις, ἐμπνεύσομαι τῇδε E.[same place]; ἄνεμος ἐμπνεύσας δορί {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyc.” 19}}; [αὐλοῖς] ἐμπνεῖν **breathe into, play** the flute, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.266 (Antip.): with _accusative_ _cognate_, Χείλεσι μοῦσαν ἐ., of Pan, “APl.” 4.226 (7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus)}}:—_passive_, ἐμπνεόμενα ὄργανα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.67; πνεῦμα -πνεόμενον τῷ αὐλῷ 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.54.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **breathe in, inhale**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φυσῶν” 4}}; but usually,
**2.b** **breathe, live, be alive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 671, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 926, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29d, etc.}}; τὰ ἐμπνέοντα, ={ἔμψυχα}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Iambi” 1.127; ἐ. τᾷ τέχνᾳ “Anthologia Graeca” 9.777 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}; of one expiring, βλέποντα κἀμπνέοντ᾽ ἔτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 883; σμικρὸν ἐμπνέουσ᾽ ἔτι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 205; βραχὺν δὴ βίοτον ἐμπνέων ἔτι [prev. author] “Hipp.” 1246.}}
**3** with _genitive_, **breathe of, be laden with**, Ἀραβίης ὀδμῆς {{NT+5th c.AD+|Perictione Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.28.19; ἐ. ἀπειλῆς καὶ φόνου NT.Act.9.1.}}
**II** _transitive_, **blow into**, ἄνεμος μέσον ἱστίον ἐ. **swell** the sail, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Bacchum” 33, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.40.}}
**II.2** **breathe into, infuse into**, μένος, θάρσος. τινί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.110, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.381, +others}}; [Μοῦσαι] ἐνέπνευσαν δέ μοι αὐδήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 31; πατρὶ.. πατρὸς ἐνέπνευσεν μένος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.70: also with}}_infinitive_ pro _accusative_, φᾶρος ἐνέπνευσε φρεσὶν ὑφαίνειν **breathed into** my mind (i.e. **inspired** me **with the thought**) to weave it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.138}}:— _passive_, **to be inspired**, ὑπὸ θεοῦ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 16.2; εἰς μαντικήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.421b.}}

1710:
** ἐμπορ-εύομαι**,
_future_ -πορεύσομαι: _aorist 1_ ἐνεπορεύθην:—_middle_, _aorist 1_ **-ευσάμην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 313d}}:—**travel**, ξένην ἔπι.. γαῖαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 456; ὡς τύραννον [prev. author] “fragment” 873}}; ποῖ δ᾽ ἐμπορεύ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 405; τηνῶθεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 754.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **walk**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 53, 5th c.BC: Metagenes Comicus 10.}}
**II** **travel for traffic** or **business**, Χρηματισμοῦ Χάριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 952e}}; εἰς Πόντον Chion {{Refs|“Ep.” 7, 8, compare “SIG” 1166 (from Dodona): _metaphorically_, ἐ. εἰς ἰατρικήν}} **inveigh against** the art of healing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τέχνης” 1.}}
**II.2** **to be a merchant, traffic**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 3.3, etc.; λόγοισιν “Comica Adespota” 269}}: with _accusative_, **trade in**, γῆν {{LXX|LXX.Gen.34.21.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **import**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 313e}}; πολλὰς διὰ θαλάσσης ὠφελείας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.86; πορφύραν ἀπὸ Φοινίκης 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.2; γλαῦκας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.Prooem.”}}
**II.3.b** _metaphorically_, δίαιταν ἥντιν᾽ ἐμπορεύεται what manner of lite he **leads**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 812.6}}; ἐ. τὴν φιλοσοφίαν **to make a trade of** it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.486, 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 23.298d, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 4.6.8; πλήθη καλῶν γυναικῶν 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.569f}}; in bad sense, **trade on**, τὴν λήθην τῶν δικαστῶν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.536.}}
**II.4** with _accusative_ _person_, **make gain of, overreach, cheat**, πλαστοῖς λόγοις ὑμᾶς {{NT|NT.2Pet.2.3}}:—also in _active_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.12.10.}}

1711:
** ἐμπορ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, (ἔμπορος) **commerce** (_accusative_ to {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1258b22, of three kinds, ναυκληρία, φορτηγία, παράστασις (qq. vv.))}}, mostly used of **commerce** or **trade by sea** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare (ἔμπορος 111), 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 646, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1166, 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 127, etc.; ἐμπορίαν ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.1; ἐμπορίας οὐκ οὔσης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2; ἐὰν κατὰ θάλατταν ἡ ἐ. γένηται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371a}}; κατ᾽ ἐμπορίην, _Attic dialect_ -ίαν, for **trade-purposes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.139, 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus [same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.4, etc.}}; ἐμπορίας ἕνεκα or -κεν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.7, 6.2; πρὸς ἐμπορίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 718: _plural_, τὰς ἐ. τὰς κερδαλέως [prev. work] 594 (_anapaest meter_)}}; περὶ τὰς ἐ. διατρίβειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1291a5, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.8.}}
**2** **a trade** or **business,** {{NT+6th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 6.63.8 (6th c.AD: Damocharis Epigrammaticus), NT.Matt.22.5.}}
**3** **errand, business**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hyps.fragment” 5.11 (_anapaest meter_), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Scyth.” 4}}; **journeying**, πενία ἀζημίωτος ἐ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 10.}}
**II** **merchandise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 3.2, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.500 (3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus); αὑτοῦ τὴν ἐ. ἔφασκεν εἶναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.25}}; ἐπὶ τῇ ἐμπορίᾳ ἢν ἦγεν ἐν τῇ.. νηΐ Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.23.}}

1712:
** ἐμπόρ-ιον**, τό,
**trading-station, mart, factory**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.165, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.100, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1523, “IPE” 12.47.9 (Olbia, 1st c.AD), etc.}}; προστάται τοῦ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.178}}; ἐ. παρέχειν, of Corinth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13.}}
**b** **market-centre** for a district which had no πόλις, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 880.22 (Macedonia, 3rd c.AD).}}
**2** τὸ ἐ., at Athens, **the Exchange**, where the merchants resorted, δανείσασθαι Χρήματα ἐν τῷ ἐμπορίῶ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.1, compare 18.309}}; ἐκ τοὐμπορίου τινές foreign **merchants**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 17.3, compare 43.9.}}
**II** **ἐμπόρια**, τά, **merchandise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 1.7.}}

1713:
** ἔμπορ-ος**, ον,
**one who goes on ship-board as a passenger**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.319, 24.300.}}
**II** = ὁ ἐν πόρῳ ὤν, **wayfarer, traveller**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.36, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 661, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 25, 303, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 999 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** **merchant, trader**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 16, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.39, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31, etc.}}; distinguished from **the retail-dealer** (κάπηλος) by his making voyages and importing goods himself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313d, “R.” 371a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1291a16, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1156: _metaphorically_, ἔ. κακῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 598}}; ἔ. βίου **a trafficker** in life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 964; ἔ. περὶ τὰ τῆς ψυχῆς μαθήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 231d}}; ὥρης ἔ. **a dealer in** beauty, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.416 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus); ἔ. γυναικῶν “IG” 14.2000.}}
**III.2** as _adjective_, ={ἐμπορικός, ναῦς ἔ}. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.12.}}

1714:
** ἐμ-πρήθω**,
**blow up, inflate**, of the wind, ἐν δ᾽ ἄνεμος πρῆσεν μέσον ἱστίον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.481}}:—_passive_, **to be bloated** or **swollen**, ἐμπεπρησμένης ὑός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 36 (-πρημ- codex R),compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.19.7.}}
**II** **burn**, ἐνέπρηθον μέγα ἄστυ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.589}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Athenaeus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 1.2.4}}; compare ἐμπίμπρημι.

1715:
** ἔμπροσθ-εν**,
sometimes also **ἔμπροσθε** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.62, 7.144, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 4.10, in Poets for the sake of meter, 3rd c.BC: Hegesippus Comicus 1.20, 3rd c.BC: Nicomachus Comicus 1.14, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.590: neither form in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus or Trag., τοὔμπροσθεν uncertain in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1228}}.
**I** _adverb_,
**I.1** of Place, **before, in front**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.126, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.23}}; τὸ and τὰ ἔ. **the front, the foreside**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HG” 2.3.55, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.62, etc.}}; εἰς τὸ ἔ. **forwards**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.61}}; στὰς ἐκ τοῦ ἔ. **in front, opposite**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.6: _metaphorically_, εἰστοὔμπ. προελθεῖν Isoc. [prev. cited]}}
**I.2** of Time, **before, of old**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 277d, etc.}}; τὰ ἔ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 448e; τὰ ἔ. τούτων ῥηθέντα [prev. author] “Lg.” 773e}}; οἱ ἔ. our **ancestors**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Plt.” 296a, Hegesippus Epigrammaticus [same place]; οἱ ἔ. Χρόνοι “PPetr.” 2p.19 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**II** as _preposition_ with _genitive_, **before, in front of**,
**II.1** of Place, ἐ. αὐτῆς (i.e. τῆς νηός) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.87, compare 2.110, etc.}}
**II.2** of Time, ἔ. ταύτης (i.e. τῆς γνώμης) {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.144}}; ἔ. εἶναι τῶν πραγμάτων to be **beforehand with** events, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.39.}}
**II.3** of Degree, ἔ. τοῦ δικαίου **preferred before** justice, {{Refs|[prev. author] 56.50.}}

1716:
** ἐμ-πτύω**,
**spit into**, ἐς ποταμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.138; εἰς στόμα ἑρπετοῦ 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.25.}}
**II** **spit upon**, εἴς τι {{NT+4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.345c; εἰς τὸ πρόσωπον “PMagd.” 24.7 (3rd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.189a; ἐς τὸ πρόσωπόν τινος 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.76, LXX.Num.12.14, NT.Matt.26.67; εἴς τινα NT.Matt.27.30: with _dative_, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 347, NT.Mark.10.24, etc.}}:—_middle_, {{LXX|LXX.Deu.25.9}}:—_passive_, **to be spat upon**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 10p.52H.}}

1717:
** ἐμφαν-ής**, ές,
**showing in** itself, **reflecting**, of mirrors, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 46a.}}
**II** **visible to the eye, manifest**,
**II.a** of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 199, etc.}}; especially of the gods **appearing bodily** among men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 22, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 733, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.141a; so ὄψις ἐ. ἐνυπνίων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 518; τέκμαρ “Ch.” 667}}; ἐ. τινὰ ἰδεῖν see him **bodily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 538, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 682; μαθεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1454}}; πῶς ἂν ὑμὶν ἐμφανὴς.. γενοίμη; how could I make it **manifest?** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 531}}; ἐμφανὴς τιμαῖσιν, ={ἐμφανῶς τιμώμενος}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 909 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐ. ζῷα **familiar** animals, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.43U.}}
**II.b** as legal term, ἐμφανῆ παρέχειν τινά to produce a person or thing **in open court**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.36, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.38}}; so ἐμφανῆ καταστῆσαι produce **in court**, either the property or the vouchers, {{Refs|[prev. author] 52.10}}; ἐμφανῶν κατάστασις, **actio ad exhibendum**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.31, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.14.}}
**II.c** of things, οὐ γάρ ἐστι τἀμφανῆ κρύπτειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 755}}; ἐ. τεκμήρια **visible** proofs, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 1109}}; ἄλγος ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 210; κλαυθμός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.111}}; μεῖξις{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 203; Χυμοί 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.3.4 (_superlative_)}}; ἐ. κόσμος **visible** sky, Vett. Val.{{Refs|8.12}}; τὰ ἐ. κτήματα the **actual** property, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.10; τοῦ μέλλοντος καὶ μὴ -οῦς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42}}; εἰς τοὐμφανὲς ἰέναι to come into **light**, come **forward**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.13}}; εἰς τοὐ. ᾠοτοκεῖν, ζῳοτοκεῖν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 510b20, 511a23}}; ἀεὶ ἐ. εἶναι to be constantly **in evidence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 9.1.}}
**II.2** **manifest, palpable**, τυραννίς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 417; βία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86}}; ἐ. λόγος a **plain** speech, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 420}}; τῷ ἐμφανεῖ λόγῳ **openly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.48; τὴν διάνοιαν ἐ. ποιεῖν διὰ φωνῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 206d; ἐμφανές ἐστιν ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 9.10.}}
**II.3** **wellknown**, τὰ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.33; ἐμφανῆ γὰρ ἦν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 448}}; **conspicuous, notable**, ἀνήρ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.68.}}
**III** _adverb_ -νῶς, _Ionic dialect_ -νέως, **visibly, openly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.140, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 626, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.48, etc.; λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 312}}; ἐ. ἐλευθεροῦν **without doubt**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.123}}; ἐ. ἠμύνατο **openly**, i.e. **not secretly** or **treacherously**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 278}}; οὐ λόγοις, ἀλλ᾽ ἐ. but **really**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 611}}: _comparative_ **-έστερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 31e.}}
**III.2** _neuter_ _adjective_, ἐκ τοῦ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.150, 4.120, +others}}; ἐν τῷ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.21, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.25.}}

1718:
** ἐμφαν-ίζω**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ **-ιῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 797}}: _perfect_ ἐμπεφάνικα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.400.2 (3rd c.BC)}}:—**show forth, manifest, exhibit**, αὐτὸς αὑτόν E.[same place],{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare 4th c.BC: Philochorus Historicus 20, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.17.2, etc.}}; ἐ. τινὰ ἐπίορκον, ἐχθρόν, **exhibit** or **represent** him as.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.12, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.36; ἐ. οὐκ οὖσαν ἀγαθὸν τὴν ἡδονήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1173b31; πᾶν σύμπτωμα 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus 10}}:— _middle_, **exhibit in court,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PMasp. 32.33 (6th c.AD)}}:—_passive_, **to become visible, be manifested**, τινί {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Wis.1.2, NT.Matt.27.53, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.107, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.13.1, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.7.}}
**2** **make clear** or **plain**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 244a, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 140, etc.}}; ἄστρα ἡμῖν τῆς νυκτὸς τὰς ὥρας ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.4: with a _relative_, τὰ παθήματα δι᾽ ἃς αἰτίας γέγονε ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 61c; ἐ. τοῦτο ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.26.}}
**3** **declare, explain**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 46a24}}; **give orders**, τινὶ ποιεῖν τι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.35.8}}; **report**, περί τινος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 412.4 (Delph., 3rd c.BC),compare “UPZ” 42.18 (2nd c.BC), “IG” 9(2).517.5 (from Larissa), “Michel” 431.6 (from Iasus)}}, etc.: —_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“GDI” 2502 B 41 (Delph., 4th c.BC).}}
**4** **lay an information against**, τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Arg. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lys.”}}:—_passive_, ὁ -ισθείς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Charondas Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.2.24.}}

1719:
** ἔμφοβ-ος**, ον,
**terrible**, θεαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 39.}}
**II** _passive_, **in fear, timorous**, ὕπειξις τῆς ψυχῆς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 25.1}}; **terrified, frightened**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.19.24, NT.Luke.24.5, +others, “Bull.Soc.Alex.” 6.45}}. _adverb_ **-βως**[{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {ὀρρωδέως}.}}]

1720:
** ἐμφῡσ-άω**,
**blow in**, ἐς τὰς ῥῖνας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.2, compare “POxy.” 1088.37}}; αὐλητρὶς ἐνεφύσησε **breathed into** the flute, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1219}}; οἴνῳ ἐ. {{Refs|“Hippiatrica” 11.}}
**II** **breathe upon**, τινί, εἴς τινα, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Job.4.21, LXX.Eze.37.9, cf. NT.John.20.22.}}
**III** **blow up, inflate**, τὸ μὲν [τῆς τροφῆς] ἐμφυσᾶν, τὸ δὲ σαρκοῦν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 603b30; ἐ. τὰς φλέβας [prev. author] “Pr.” 881b14}}:—_passive_, **to be inflated** or, generally, **swollen**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 154, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 524a17, +others: _metaphorically_, τῇ κολακείᾳ ἐμφυσώμενος 4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 25.}}

1721:
** ἔμφῠτος**, ον,
**inborn, natural**, ἔ. μαντικὴν εἶχε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.94; πατρὸς αἶμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1671 (Lyric poetry); τοῖς πλουτοῦσι τοῦτο δ᾽ ἔ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 776.1, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 15.1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; ἔρως ἔ. τοῖς ἀνθρώποις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 191d}}; ἡ μὲν [ἰδέα] ἔ. οὖσα, ἐπιθυμία ἡδονῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 237d, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.1}}; αἰσχροκέρδεια, πονηρία, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.108; κακία LXX.Wis.12.10}}; ἔ. ἡ ἀρετή, opposed to διδακτός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 398c, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 33.7; τὸ ἔ. θερμόν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.14; ἔ. θερμότης 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 355b9}}; οὐκ ἦν ταῦτα τοῖς Ἀθηναίοις πάτρια.. οὐδ᾽ ἔ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.203; τὰν ἔ. αὐτοῖς ἀθεσίαν “IPE” 1.185 (from Chersonesus)}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus “Fragmenta” 70H.}}
**II** **planted**, Χωρίον {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PHamb.” 23.16 (6th c.AD)}}; ἐλαῖαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 241.28 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** **implanted**, λόγος {{NT|NT.Jam.1.21.}}

1722:
** ἐν**,
_poetry_ **ἐνί**, **εἰν**, **εἰνί** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.199, etc.)}}, forms used by _Epic dialect_ and Lyric Poets as the metre requires, but only as _falsa lectio_ in Trag., εἰν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1241; εἰνί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 893: 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus and 5th c.AD: Cypria}} **ἰν** {{Refs|“IG” 5(2).3.5, +others, “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.9 H., +others}}
PREP. WITH DAT. AND ACC. Radical sense, **in, into.**
**A** WITH DAT.
**A.I** OF PLACE,
**A.I.1** **in**, νήσῳ ἐν ἀμφιρύτῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.50; ἐν δώμασ᾽ ἐμοῖσιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.221; ἐνὶ προθύροισιν 11.777; κοίλῃσ᾽ ἐνὶ νηυσί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.27}}; with names of cities or islands, as ἐν Ἀθήνῃς, ἐν Τροίῃ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.549, 162; ἡ ἐν Κερκύρᾳ ναυμαχία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.57; ἡ ἐν Σαλαμῖνι μάχη 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.147 (but in _Attic dialect_ the _preposition_ is sometimes omitted, as with Ἐλευσῖνι, Μαραθῶνι}}; where ἐν is used, it = **in the district of**.., ὲν Ἐλευσῖνι {{Refs|“IG” 22.1028.11, ἐμ Μαραθῶνι [prev. work]1243.21): ἐν χερσὶν ἐμῇσι}} **in** my arms, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.426; ἐνὶ θυμῷ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.331, etc.}}; ἐν αὑτῷ εἶναι to be **in** one's senses, be oneself, ἔτ᾽ ἐν σαυτῷ (variant -τοῦ) γενοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 950; also ἐν αὑτοῦ, compare signification 2.}}
**A.I.1.b** ἐν τοῖς ἰχθύσιν **in** the fish-market, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 125; ἐν τῷ μύρῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1375}}; so ἐν τοῖν δυοῖν ὀβολοῖν ἐθεώρουν ἄν **in** the two-obol seats, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.28.}}
**A.I.2** elliptic, in such phrases as ἐν Ἀλκινόοιο {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.132, compare “Leg.Gort.” 2.21, etc.; εἰν Ἀΐδαο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.389, _Attic dialect_ ἐν Ἅιδου (see. Ἅιδης): later ἐν τοῖς τινός “PRev.Laws” 38.1 (3rd c.BC), NT.Luke.2.49; ἐν ἡμετέρου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.35, 7.8. δ; ἐμ Πανδίονος “IG” 22.1138.8; ἐν Δημοτιωνιδῶν [prev. work]2.841b21; ἐν τῶν πόλεων [prev. work] 12.56.14: mostly with _proper name_, but sometimes with Appellatives, as, ἐν ἀφνειοῦ πατρός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.47; ἐν ἀνδρὸς εὐσεβεστάτου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 926}}; ἐν παιδοτρίβου, ἐν κιθαριστοῦ, **at the school of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 973, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 206a}}; ἐν γειτόνων (see. γείτων) ἐν αὑτοῦ (αὑτῷ codex Rav.) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 642, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 125; οὐκέτ᾽ ἐν ἐμαυτοῦ ἦν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 155d; ἐν ὑμῶν αὐτῶν γένεσθε 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 35.15.}}
**A.I.3** **in, within, surrounded by**, οὐρανὸς ἐν αἰθέρι καὶ νεφέλῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.192; after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, of clothing, armour, etc., ἐν ἐσθῆτι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.159; ἐν πεπλώματι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 613; ἐν ἔντεσι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.24}}; ἐν ὅπλοισι **in** or **under** arms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.13, etc.}}; also of particular kinds of arms, ἐν τόξοις, ἀκοντίοις, etc., **equipped with** them, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.9.2; ἐν μαχαίρῃ “PTeb.” 16.14 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἐν μεγάλοις φορτίοις βαδίζειν καὶ τρέχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.14; ἐν βαθεῖ πώγωνι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 5.}}
**A.I.4** **on, at** or **by**, ἐν ποταμῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.521, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.466; ἐν ὄρεσσιν 19.205; οὔρεος ἐν κορυφῇς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.456; ἐν θρόνοις 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.422}}; νευρὴ ἐν τόξῳ the string **on** the bow, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.463; ἐν [ξίφει] ἧλοι 11.29}}; κατεκλάσθη ἐνὶ καυλῷ ἔγχος was broken off **at** or **by** the shaft,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|13.608; ἐν πέτροισι πέτρον ἐκτρίβων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 296}}; ἐν οἴνῳ **at** wine, probably in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 23, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 15.}}
**A.I.5** **in the number of, amongst**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἐν Δαναοῖσι, προμάχοισι, μέσσοισιν, νεκύεσσι, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.109, 3.31, 7.384, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.383, +others; οἴη ἐν ἀθανάτοισιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.398}}; and with Verbs of ruling, ἐν δ᾽ ἄρα τοῖσιν ἦρχ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|13.689; ἀνδράσιν ἐν πολλοῖσι.. ἀνάσσων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.110; φῦλον ἐν ἀνθρώποισι ματαιότατον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.21; ἐν τοῖς οἰκείοισιν ἀνὴρ χρηστός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 661; ἐν γυναιξὶν ἄλκιμος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 754:}} —for ἐν τοῖς with _superlative_, V. ὁ.
**A.I.5.b** **in the presence of**, ἐν πᾶσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.194; πτωχὸς ὢν ἐν ἐσθλοῖσιν λέγειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 703; λέγοντες ἐν τῷ δήμῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 565b; μακρηγορεῖν ἐν εἰδόσι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.36; ἔλεγον ἐν τοῖς τριάκοντα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.6; ἐν τοῖς ὄχλοις εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.21}}; λέγειν ἐν ἀνδράσιν (of a woman) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.11}}; of a trial, διαγωνίζεσθαι, διαδικάζεσθαι ἔν τισι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 464d, “Lg.” 916b; προὐκαλούμην ἐν τοῖς αὐτοῖς δικασταῖς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.23.}}
**A.I.6** **in one's hands, within one's reach** or **power**, νίκης πείρατ᾽ ἔχονται ἐν ἀθανάτοισι θεοῖσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.102; δύναμις γὰρ ἐν ὑμῖν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.69 (comparative the Homeric phrases θεῶν ἐν γούνασι κεῖται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.514; ἐν γὰρ χερσὶ τέλος πολέμου 16.630); frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus and _Attic dialect_, ἔστιν ἔν τινι}}, with _infinitive_, it depends **on** him to.., rests **with** him to.., ἔστιν ἐν σοὶ ἢ.. ἤ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, compare 3.85, etc.; ταῦτα δ᾽ ἐν τῷ δαίμονι καὶ τῇδε φῦναι χἁτέρᾳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1443; ἐν σοὶ γάρ ἐσμεν [prev. author] “OT” 314; ἐν σοὶ δ᾽ ἐσμὲν καὶ ζῆν καὶ μή 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 278; ἐν ταῖς ναυσὶ τῶν Ἑλλήνων τὰ πράγματα ἐγένετο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.74; ἐν τῷ θεῷ τὸ τέλος ἦν, οὐκ ἐμοί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.193}}; also ἐν τούτῳ εἰσὶν πᾶσαι αἱ ἀποδείξεις depend **on** this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 354e; ἐν τούτῳ λύεται ἡ ἀπορία ἢ ἄλλοθι οὐδαμοῦ [prev. work] 321e}}; ἔν γ᾽ ἐμοί so far as rests **with** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 153 (Lyric poetry); ἐν δὲ σοὶ λελείψομαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 324}}; also ἐν ἐμοί **in** my judgement, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1214 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐν θεοῖς καλά **in the eyes of** the gods, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 925.}}
**A.I.7** **in respect of**, ἐν πάντεσσ᾽ ἔργοισι δαήμονα φῶτα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.671}}; ἐν γήρᾳ σύμμετρός τινι **in point of** age.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1112}}; ἐν ἐμοὶ θρασύς **in** my case, **towards** me, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1315; ἐν θανοῦσιν ὑβριστής [prev. work] 1092}}; ἡ ἐν τοῖς ὅπλοις μάθησις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 190d}}; also οὐδὲν δεινὸν μὴ ἐν ἐμοὶ στῇ stop **with** me, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 28b.}}
**A.I.8** in a pregnant construction with Verbs of motion, **into**; implying both **motion to** and subsequent **position in** a place, ἐν κονίῃσι χαμαὶ πέσεν **fell** [to the dust and lay] **in** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.482, etc.; βάλον ἐν κονίῃσι 5.588; νηῒ δ᾽ ἐνὶ πρύμνῃ ἔναρα θῆκ᾽ 10.570; ἐν χερσὶ τιθέναι 1.441, etc.; ἐν χερσὶ βαλεῖν 5.574}}; ἐν στήθεσσι μένος βαλεῖν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 513; ἐν Τρωσὶν ὄρουσαν 16.258; ἐν χερσὶ πεσέειν 6.81; λέων ἐν βουσὶ θορών 5.161; ἐν δ᾽ οἶνον ἔχευεν ἐν δέπαϊ χρυσέῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.261; ἐν τεύχεσσιν ἔδυνον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.131}}: in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ἐν ποίμναις πίτνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 184 (Lyric poetry), compare 374 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐν χωρίῳ ἐμπεπτωκώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.87; ἡ ἐν τῷ Σπειραίῳ τῶν νεῶν καταφυγή [prev. author] 8.11; ἐν τόπῳ καταπεφευγέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 260c; ἐν ᾅδου διαπορευθείς [prev. author] “Lg.” 905b; ῥιπτοῦντες σφᾶς ἐν τῇ θαλάσσῃ 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.19.4}}; later, with Verbs of **coming** and **going**, διαβάντες ἐν τῇ Σάμῳ {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 7.4.3, cf. LXX.Tob.5.5, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.11.32, etc.: τὸν ἐν Σικελίᾳ πλοῦν}} is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.43 codices}}
**A.I.9** πίνειν ἐν ποτηρίῳ to drink **from** a cup, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 6.2; ἐν ἀργύρῳ πίνειν [prev. author] “Merc.Cond.” 26; ἐν μικροῖς 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.104.}}
**A.I.10** ἄργυρος ἐν ἐκπώμασι silver **in the form of** plate, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.260a}}; ἐμ φέρνῃ, ἐν θέματι, **as** a dowry, pledge, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PPetr.” 1p.37, “PTeb.” 120.125 (1st c.BC).}}
**A.I.11** in citations, ἐν τοῦ σκήπτρου τῇ παραδόσει **in** the passage of the {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” describing this, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147c, “Phlb.” 33b.}}
**A.II** OF STATE, CONDITION or POSITION:
**A.II.1** of outward circumstances, ἐν πολέμῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.553; ἐν δαιτί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.259; ἐν καρὸς αἴσῃ 9.378; ἐν μοίρῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.54; οὑμὸς ἐν φάει βίος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1281}}; ἐν γένει εἶναί τινι to be related to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1016}}; of occupations, pursuits, ἐν φιλοσοφίᾳ εἶναι to be engaged **in** philosophy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a, compare “R.” 489b}}; οἱ ἐν ποιήσι γενόμενοι poets, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.82}}; οἱ ἐν τοῖς πράγμασι ministers of state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.28}}; οἱ ἐν τέλει the magistrates, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.73, etc.; τοὺς ἐν ταῖς μοναρχίαις ὄντας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.5; ὁ ἐν ταῖς προσόδοις “PPetr.” 1p.62}}; ὁ μάντις ἦν ἐν τῇ τέχνῃ **in the practice of** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 562.}}
**A.II.2** of inward states, of feeling, etc., ἐν φιλότητι, ἐν δοιῇ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.302, 9.230; ἐν φόβῳ γενέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 578e; οὐκ ἐν αἰσχύνῃ τὰ σά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1276; ἐν σιωπῇ τἀμά [prev. author] “Ion” 1397}}; ἐν ὀργῇ ἔχειν τινά to make him the object of one's anger, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.21; ἐν ἔριδι εἶναι [prev. passage]; ἐν αἰτίᾳ σχεῖν τινά}} to blame him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.106; ἐν αἰτίᾳ βαλεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 656 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐν αἰτίᾳ εἶναι to have the blame, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.8.9, etc.; οἱ ἐν ταῖς αἰτίαις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 2.14.}}
**A.II.3** frequently with _neuter_ _adjective_, ἐν βραχεῖ, ={βραχέως}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 673}}; ἐν τάχει, ={ταχέως}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 765, etc.}}; ἐν καλῷ ἐστί, ={καλῶς ἔχει}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 971}}; ἐν ἀσφαλεῖ [ἐστί] {{Refs|[prev. author] “IT” 762}}; ἐν εὐμαρεῖ [ἐστί] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hel.” 1227; ἐν ἐλαφρῷ ποιήσασθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.154}}; ἐν ἴσῳ, ={ἴσως, ἐν ὁμοίῳ}, ={ὁμοίως}, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53: less frequently in _plural_, ἐν ἀργοῖς, ={ἀργῶς}, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 287}}]; ἐν κενοῖς, ={κενῶς}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 971: with a}} _substantive_, ἐν δίκᾳ, ={δικαίως}, opposed to παρὰ δίκαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.16, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1069, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 258, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 475c, +others; ἦσαν οὐκέτι ὁμοίως ἐν ἡδονῇ ἄρχοντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 977b.}}
**A.III** OF THE INSTRUMENT, MEANS or MANNER, ἐν πυρὶ πρήσαντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.429; δῆσαι ἐνὶ δεσμῷ 5.386, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.54, etc.}}; but in most cases the originally sense may be traced, to put **in** the fire and burn, **in**fetters and bind, etc.; so ἐν πόνοις δαμέντα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 425 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἔζευξα πρῶτος ἐν ζυγοῖσι κνώδαλα{{Refs|[prev. work] 462; ἔργον ἐν κύβοις Ἄρης κρινεῖ [prev. author] “Th.” 414}}; also ἐν ὀφθαλμοῖσιν or ἐν ὄμμασιν ὁρᾶσθαι, ἰδέσθαι, to see **with** or **before** one's eyes, i.e. have the object **in** one's eye, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.306, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.385, etc.; ἔν τε τῇ ὄψει διαγιγνώσκειν καὶ ἐν τῇ ἀκοῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 206a; also ἐν ὠσὶ νωμῶν ὄρνιθας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 25}}; also ἐν λιταῖς **by** prayers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 60}}; ἐν δόλῳ **by** deceit, {{Refs|[prev. work] 102}}; ἐν λόγοις **by** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 613 (Lyric poetry); ἀπέκτειναν ἐν τῇ προφάσει ταύτῃ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.12, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.59; ψαύειν ἐν κερτομίοις γλώσσαις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 961 (Lyric poetry); ἐν τοῖς ὁμοίοις νόμοις ποιήσαντες τὰς κρίσεις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77}}; especially with Verbs of showing, σημαίνειν ἐν ἱεροῖς καὶ οἰωνοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.3}}; τὰ πραχθέντα.. ἐν.. ἐπιστολαῖς ἴστε ye know **by** letters, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.11; ἐν τῇδε ῥάβδῳ πάντα ποιήσεις 2nd c.BC: Ezekiel Poeta Judaeus “Exagoge - Mnemosyne” 132, compare “PMag.Osl.” 1.108.}}
**A.III.2** of a personal instrument, ἐν τῷ ἄρχοντι τῶν δαιμονίων ἐκβάλλει τὰ δαιμόνια {{NT|NT.Matt.9.34.}}
**A.IV** OF TIME, ὥρῃ ἐν εἰαρινῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.643; ἐν νυκτί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.69, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.9; ἐν χρόνῳ μακρῷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 235, “OC” 88}}; ἐν τούτῳ (i.e. τῷ χρόνῶ) **in** this space of time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, etc.}}; ἐν ᾧ (i.e. χρόνῳ) **during the time** that, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 929, etc. (also ἐν οἷς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 391a2); ἐν ὅσῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.28}}; ἐν ταῖς σπονδαῖς **in the time of** the truce, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.1; ἐν τῇ ἑορτῇ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.73 (but in some phrases the ἐν is omitted, as μυστηρίοις}} **in the course of** the mysteries, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1013}}; τραγῳδοῖς **at the performance of**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.36).}}
**A.IV.b** ἐν ἄρχοντι Μητροδώρῳ **during** the archonship of M., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 7.1773 (Thebes, 2nd c.AD); ἐν ἄρχοντι Σύλλᾳ [prev. work]3.113.}}
**A.IV.2** **in, within**, ἐν ἡμέρῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126; ἐν ἔτεσι πεντήκοντα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118; ἐν τρισὶ μησί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.37, etc.}}; μυρίαις ἐν ἁμέραις **in**, i.e. **after**, countless days, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 305 (Lyric poetry); ἐν ἡμέραις πολλαῖς νοσῆσαι 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 9.35.}}
**A.V** OF NUMBERS generally, ἐν δυσὶ σταδίοις **within** two stadia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.74, compare 19.39, uncertain in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.1.}}
**A.V.2** with _genitive_ of price, ἐν δύο ταλάντων {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.16.24.}}
**A.V.3** **amounting to**, προῖκα ἐν δραχμαῖς ἐννακοσίαις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 970.14 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**B** WITH Acc., **into, on, for**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ἰν, νόμος ἰν ἄματα πάντα “IG” 5 (2).5; γράψαι ἐν χάλκωμα [prev. work]511; ἐν πελτοφόρας ἀπεγράψατο [prev. work]7.210 (from Aegosthenae), etc.}}; also _poetry_, ἐν πάντα νόμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.86.}}
**C** WITHOUT CASE, AS _adverb_, in the phrase ἐν δέ..,
**C.1** **and therein**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.361; ἐν μέν.. ἐν δέ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.244.}}
**C.2** **and among them**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.588, etc.; in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, mostly ἐν δὲ δή.. 3.39, 5.95; or ἐν δὲ καί.. 2.43, 172, 176.}}
**C.3** **and besides, moreover** (not in _Attic dialect_ Prose), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 675, “OT” 181 (Lyric poetry), +others; ἐν δ᾽ ὑπέρας τε κάλους τε πόδας τ᾽ ἐνέδησεν ἐν αὐτῇ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.260.}}
**C.4** ἔνι, ={ἔνεστι, ἔνεισι}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.248, etc.}}
**D** POSITION: ἐν frequently stands between its _substantive_ and the _adjective_ agreeing therewith, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.61, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.41, etc.}}: without an _adjective_, τῷ δ᾽ ἐν ἐρινεός ἐστι μέγας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.103: most frequently in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in the form ἐνί, which is then written by anastrophe ἔνι, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.221, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.57; in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}} between _substantive_ and _genitive_, χόρτοις ἐν λέοντος {{Refs|“O.” 13.44, +others}}--One or more independent words sometimes come between the _preposition_ and its _dative_, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.115}}; also in Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.69.}}
**E** IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words),
**E.I** with Verbs, the _preposition_ mostly retains its sense of being **in** or **at** a place, etc., with _dative_, or followed by εἰς.., or ἐν..: in such forms as ἐνορᾶν τινί τι, in translating, we resolve the _compound_, to remark a thing **in** one.
**E.I.b** also, **at** a person, ἐγγελᾶν, ἐνυβρίζειν τινί.
**E.I.2** with _adjectives_, it expresses
**E.I.2.a** a modified degree, as in ἔμπηλος, ἔμπικρος, ἔνσιμος, **rather**...
**E.I.2.b** the possession of a quality, as in ἔναιμος **with** blood **in it**, ἐνάκανθος thorny: ἔμφωνος **with** a voice: ἔννομος **in accordance with** law, etc.
**E.II** ἐν becomes ἐμ- before the labials β μ π φ ; ἐγ- before the gutturals γ κ ξ ; ἐλ- before ; ἐρ- before ; rarely ἐσ- before ; but Inscrr. and Papyri often preserve ἐν- in all these cases.

1723:
** ἐναγκᾰλ-ίζομαι**,
_middle_, **take in one's arms,** {{LXX+1st c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 7.476.10 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), LXX.Pro.24.48; τέκνα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 5, “IG” 12(7).395.25 (from Amorgos): _metaphorically_, Νεῖλος [πόλιν] ἐ. 5th-6th c.AD: Procopius Gazaeus Rhetor “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 133}}; of a science, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Cit. 3.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be taken in the arms**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.58.}}

1726:
** ἐναντίος**, α, ον,
**opposite**, ={ἀντίος} (which is rare in Prose):
**1** of Place, **on the opposite side, opposite**, c._dative_, ἀκταὶ ἐναντίαι ἀλλήλῃσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.89; Πάτροκλος δέ οἱ.. ἐ. ἧστο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.190, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.89}}: hence, **fronting, face to face**, αὐτῳ οὔ πω φαίνετ᾽ ἐναντίη {{Refs 7th c.BC+|6.329; ὄττις ἐ. τοι ἰσδάνει 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.2}}; δεῖξον.. τὸ σὸν πρόσωπον δεῦρ᾽ ἐ. πατρί **before** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 947}}; τὰναντία τινί **things open to** one's **sight**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.345: _absolutely_, ἐ. στάνθ᾽ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1078 (but ἐ. κεῖσθαι}} look **opposite** ways, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190a).}}
**1.b** with Verbs of motion, **in the opposite direction**, ἔνθα οἱ.. ἐναντίη ἤλυθε μήτηρ came **to meet** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.251; ἐναντίοι ἀλλήλοισιν ὄγμον ἐλαύνωσιν 11.67; δύο ἅμαξαι ἐ. ἀλλήλαις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93; ἄνεμος ἐ. ἔπνει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.3.}}
**1.c** Astrology texts, **in diametrical aspect**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 70.16, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 3.360.}}
**2** in hostile sense, **opposing, facing** in fight, with _genitive_, ἐναντίοι ἔσταν Ἀχαιῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.497, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1284, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.28, etc.: with _dative_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.12, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 856, “IT” 1415}}; οἱ ἐ. **one's adversaries**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 375, 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 12 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.}}; **the enemy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.225, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.64, etc.}}
**2.b** generally, **opposed to**, τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.10}}; τὸ ἐ. **the opposite party**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ath.” 1.4}}; **presenting obstacles, hindering**, τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 642.}}
**2.c** ὁ δι ἐναντίας the **opponent** in a alawsuit, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 1.58.15 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**3** of qualities, acts, etc., **opposite, contrary, reverse**, τἀναντί᾽ εἰπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1373; δίκαια καὶ τἀναντία 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 667: mostly with _genitive_, τὰ ἐ. τούτων}} the **very reverse** of these things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75, etc.}}; δείξας.. ἄστρων τὴν ἐ. ὁδόν, i.e. τὴν τοῦ ἡλίου ὁδὸν ἐ. οὖσαν τοῖς ἄστροις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 861: also with _dative_, Ὀρφεῖ δὲ γλῶσσαν τὴν ἐναντίαν ἔχεις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1629}}; τἀναντία πρήσσειν [τῇ ὑγιείῃ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 234; δύο τὰ -ώτατα εὐβουλίᾳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42; ἀγαθῷ κακὸν -ώτερον ἢ τῷ μὴ ἀγαθῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 491d; ἐναντία λέγει αὐτὸς αὑτῷ [prev. author] “Prt.” 339b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 493; τἀναντία τούτοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt” 323d}}; ἐναντία γνῶναι ταῖς πλείσταις [πόλεσιν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 1.2; τὴν ἐ. τινὶ ψῆφον θέσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.65; simply τὴν ἐ. θέσθαι τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 184d: followed by ἤ, τοὺς ἐ. λόγους ἢ ὡς αὐτὸς κατεδόκεε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22; τοὐ. δρῶν ἢ προσῆκ᾽ αὐτῷ ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 14; τοὐ. ἔπαθεν ἢ τὸ προσδοκώμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 966e, compare “R.” 567c, etc.: frequently strengthened, πᾶν τοὐ., πάντα τἀ}}., quite **the contrary, Lg.**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|967a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.12.4}}; πολὺ τοὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 57; τὸ δὲ πολὺ ἐναντίον ἀποβήσεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39c.}}
**3.b** τὰ ἐ. **opposites** in Philos., {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus 3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 986b3, etc.}}
**4** in the Philos. of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, τἀναντία (distinct from other ἀντικείμενα, “Metaphorical” 1018a25)}} are **contraries**, especially the two attributes within the same genus which differ most widely from each other (as hot and cold), {{Refs|“Cat.” 6a18, +others}}
**4.b** ἐ. ἀποφάνσεις, προτάσεις, **contrary** propositions (All {{Refs|B is A, No B is A)}}, opposed to **contradictory** (see. ἀντιφατικῶς), {{Refs|[prev. author] “Int.” 17b4, “APr.” 63b28.}}
**II** frequently in _adverb_ usages:
**II.1** from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards, _neuter_ ἐναντίον}} as _adverb_, **opposite, facing**, ἐ. ὧδε κάλεσσον here **to my face**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.544}}; εἰς ὦπα ἰδέσθαι ἐ. to look one **in the face**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.107; ἐ. προσβλέπειν τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 968, etc.; γυναῖκας ἀνδρῶν μὴ βλέπειν ἐ. [prev. work] 975: _absolutely_, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.40, etc.}}: hence, like a _preposition_ with _genitive_, **in the presence of**, τῆς βουλῆς {{Refs|“IG” 12.91}}; τῶνδ᾽ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1002}}; μαρτύρων ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 448; ἐ. τοῦ παιδίου [prev. author] “Lys.” 907; ἐ. ἁπάντων λέγειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.25; ἐ. Διός 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.9.2; also _neuter_ _plural_, “IG” 7.1779 (from Thespiae).}}
**II.1.b** in hostile sense, **against**, with _genitive_, ἀνέσταν.. σφοῦ πατρὸς ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.534; ἐ. ἰέναι τινός 21.574; ἐ. μάχεσθαί τινος 20.97; ἐ. ἵστασ᾽ ἐμεῖο 13.448: _absolutely_, ἐ. μίμνειν}} stand one's ground **against**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 106: with _dative_, νεικεῖν ἀλλήλοισιν ἐ. 20.252; ἐ. θεοῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 624; ἐ. τῷ ὅρκῳ πράττειν “IG” 22.1258.2.}}
**II.1.c** **contrariwise**, in _Attic dialect_ also with the _Article_, τοὐναντίον **on the other hand**, τοὐ. δέ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 80.4}}; ἢ πάλιν τοὐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 460.5}}; **conversely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89e.}}
**II.1.d** _neuter_ _plural_ ἐναντία as _adverb_, with _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.32, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.29, etc.}}
**II.2** with Preps., ἐκ τοῦ ἐ. **over against, opposite**, opposed to ἐκ πλαγίου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.15, etc.}}; ἐξ ἐναντίας, _Ionic dialect_ -ίης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.225, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.33 (οἱ ἐξ ἐ}}. the **opposing parties**, probably in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.78.26 (4th c.AD))}}; ἐκ τῶν ἐ. **on the contrary**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.9.9; ἀπ᾽ ἐναντίας 1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 1.2; ἀπ᾽ ἐ. Χωρεῖν 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 4}}; κατὰ τὰ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 39a: Geometry texts, αἱ κατ᾽ ἐναντίον τοῦ παραλληλογράμμου πλευραί}} the **opposite** sides of the parallelogram, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἰσορροπιῶν” 1.9}}; αἱ κατ᾽ ἐ. τομαί **opposite** sections (i. e. branches) of the hyperbola, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 3.23.}}
**II.3** regular _adverb_ -ίως **contrariwise**, with _dative_, τούτοις οὐκ ἐ. λέγεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 642; ἐ. διακεῖσθαί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361c; ἐ. ἀντικεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Interpretatione” 17b20}}; πικρῶς καὶ ἐ. **like an enemy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.339; ἐ. ἢ ὡς ἀνδραπόδοις τραφεῖσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175d}}; ἐ. ἔχειν to be **exactly opposed**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 278a}}; πρός τι to be **contrary** in respect of.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.4}}; in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, “Metaphorical” 1057b11, +others, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Alcibiadem” p.268C.}}

