**2.b** generally, **opposed to**, τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.10}}; τὸ ἐ. **the opposite party**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ath.” 1.4}}; **presenting obstacles, hindering**, τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 642.}}
**2.c** ὁ δι ἐναντίας the **opponent** in a alawsuit, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 1.58.15 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**3** of qualities, acts, etc., **opposite, contrary, reverse**, τἀναντί᾽ εἰπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1373; δίκαια καὶ τἀναντία 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 667: mostly with _genitive_, τὰ ἐ. τούτων}} the **very reverse** of these things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75, etc.}}; δείξας.. ἄστρων τὴν ἐ. ὁδόν, i.e. τὴν τοῦ ἡλίου ὁδὸν ἐ. οὖσαν τοῖς ἄστροις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 861: also with _dative_, Ὀρφεῖ δὲ γλῶσσαν τὴν ἐναντίαν ἔχεις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1629}}; τἀναντία πρήσσειν [τῇ ὑγιείῃ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 234; δύο τὰ -ώτατα εὐβουλίᾳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42; ἀγαθῷ κακὸν -ώτερον ἢ τῷ μὴ ἀγαθῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 491d; ἐναντία λέγει αὐτὸς αὑτῷ [prev. author] “Prt.” 339b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 493; τἀναντία τούτοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt” 323d}}; ἐναντία γνῶναι ταῖς πλείσταις [πόλεσιν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 1.2; τὴν ἐ. τινὶ ψῆφον θέσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.65; simply τὴν ἐ. θέσθαι τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 184d: followed by ἤ, τοὺς ἐ. λόγους ἢ ὡς αὐτὸς κατεδόκεε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22; τοὐ. δρῶν ἢ προσῆκ᾽ αὐτῷ ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 14; τοὐ. ἔπαθεν ἢ τὸ προσδοκώμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 966e, compare “R.” 567c, etc.: frequently strengthened, πᾶν τοὐ., πάντα τἀ}}., quite **the contrary, Lg.**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|967a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.12.4}}; πολὺ τοὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 57; τὸ δὲ πολὺ ἐναντίον ἀποβήσεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39c.}}
**3.b** τὰ ἐ. **opposites** in Philos., {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus 3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 986b3, etc.}}
**4** in the Philos. of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, τἀναντία (distinct from other ἀντικείμενα, “Metaphorical” 1018a25)}} are **contraries**, especially the two attributes within the same genus which differ most widely from each other (as hot and cold), {{Refs|“Cat.” 6a18, +others}}
**4.b** ἐ. ἀποφάνσεις, προτάσεις, **contrary** propositions (All {{Refs|B is A, No B is A)}}, opposed to **contradictory** (see. ἀντιφατικῶς), {{Refs|[prev. author] “Int.” 17b4, “APr.” 63b28.}}
**II** frequently in _adverb_ usages:
**II.1** from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards, _neuter_ ἐναντίον}} as _adverb_, **opposite, facing**, ἐ. ὧδε κάλεσσον here **to my face**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.544}}; εἰς ὦπα ἰδέσθαι ἐ. to look one **in the face**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.107; ἐ. προσβλέπειν τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 968, etc.; γυναῖκας ἀνδρῶν μὴ βλέπειν ἐ. [prev. work] 975: _absolutely_, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.40, etc.}}: hence, like a _preposition_ with _genitive_, **in the presence of**, τῆς βουλῆς {{Refs|“IG” 12.91}}; τῶνδ᾽ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1002}}; μαρτύρων ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 448; ἐ. τοῦ παιδίου [prev. author] “Lys.” 907; ἐ. ἁπάντων λέγειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.25; ἐ. Διός 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.9.2; also _neuter_ _plural_, “IG” 7.1779 (from Thespiae).}}
**II.1.b** in hostile sense, **against**, with _genitive_, ἀνέσταν.. σφοῦ πατρὸς ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.534; ἐ. ἰέναι τινός 21.574; ἐ. μάχεσθαί τινος 20.97; ἐ. ἵστασ᾽ ἐμεῖο 13.448: _absolutely_, ἐ. μίμνειν}} stand one's ground **against**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 106: with _dative_, νεικεῖν ἀλλήλοισιν ἐ. 20.252; ἐ. θεοῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 624; ἐ. τῷ ὅρκῳ πράττειν “IG” 22.1258.2.}}
**II.1.c** **contrariwise**, in _Attic dialect_ also with the _Article_, τοὐναντίον **on the other hand**, τοὐ. δέ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 80.4}}; ἢ πάλιν τοὐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 460.5}}; **conversely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89e.}}
**II.1.d** _neuter_ _plural_ ἐναντία as _adverb_, with _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.32, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.29, etc.}}
**II.2** with Preps., ἐκ τοῦ ἐ. **over against, opposite**, opposed to ἐκ πλαγίου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.15, etc.}}; ἐξ ἐναντίας, _Ionic dialect_ -ίης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.225, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.33 (οἱ ἐξ ἐ}}. the **opposing parties**, probably in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.78.26 (4th c.AD))}}; ἐκ τῶν ἐ. **on the contrary**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.9.9; ἀπ᾽ ἐναντίας 1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 1.2; ἀπ᾽ ἐ. Χωρεῖν 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 4}}; κατὰ τὰ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 39a: Geometry texts, αἱ κατ᾽ ἐναντίον τοῦ παραλληλογράμμου πλευραί}} the **opposite** sides of the parallelogram, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἰσορροπιῶν” 1.9}}; αἱ κατ᾽ ἐ. τομαί **opposite** sections (i. e. branches) of the hyperbola, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 3.23.}}
**II.3** regular _adverb_ -ίως **contrariwise**, with _dative_, τούτοις οὐκ ἐ. λέγεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 642; ἐ. διακεῖσθαί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361c; ἐ. ἀντικεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Interpretatione” 17b20}}; πικρῶς καὶ ἐ. **like an enemy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.339; ἐ. ἢ ὡς ἀνδραπόδοις τραφεῖσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175d}}; ἐ. ἔχειν to be **exactly opposed**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 278a}}; πρός τι to be **contrary** in respect of.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.4}}; in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, “Metaphorical” 1057b11, +others, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Alcibiadem” p.268C.}}

1728:
** ἐνάρχομαι**,
_future_ -ξομαι probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus (see. below)}}:—in sacrifices, **begin the offering**, by taking the barley from the basket, κανᾶ δ᾽ ἐναρχέσθω τις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1470, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 7; προχύτας Χέρνιβάς τ᾽ ἐνάρξεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 955}}: _perfect_ in _passive_ sense, κανοῦν δ᾽ ἐνῆρκται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 1142; ἐνῆρκται τὰ κανᾶ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.120.}}
**2** generally, **begin,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Sammelb. 4369(b).23 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; τῆς θερείας ἐναρχομένης {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.30.7: with}}_infinitive_, πολεμεῖν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.5; γενειᾶν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.13: ἐ. τινός}} **make a beginning of**, τῆς ἐπιβολῆς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.1.3; τοῦ λόγου 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 35}}; ὁμιλιῶν **engage in**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Athenaeus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus (?) 21.9}}; ἐνῆρκται followed by a quotation, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Apollonius Citiensis Medicus 1}}: _absolutely_, **begin to speak**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 32.}}
**II** later, in _active_, **hold office,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(5).526.5 (from Ceos).}}

1729:
** ἐνδε-ής**, ές,
_neuter_ _plural_ ἐνδεᾶ: (ἐνδέω B):— **wanting** or **lacking in, in need of**, with _genitive_, ἐ. εἶναι or γεγενῆσθαί τινος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.77; ἑνός μοι μῦθος ἐ. ἔτι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 835}}; πολλῶν ἐ., opposed to αὐτάρκης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369b}}; ποιητοῦ δ᾽ ἐστὶν ἐ... πρὸς τὸ ἐπιδεῖξαι, **caret vate sacro**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 195d, compare “Lg.” 697e; σμικροῦ τινος ἐ. εἰμι πάντ᾽ ἔχειν [prev. author] “Prt.” 329b.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **in want, in need**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.3 codices, etc.}}
**2.b** **lacking, deficient**, frequently in _comparative_, ἐνδεέστερα πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.48}}; φαίνεται ἐνδεεστέρα [ἡ στρατεία] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10; -εστέρα παρασκευή [prev. author] 4.65; ἐνδεέστεροι ταῖς οὐσίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.105}}; also in Posit., οὐδὲν ἐνδεὲς ποιεῖσθαι leave nothing **unsaid**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 375; τοῦτο πολλῷ τοῦ παρόντος ἐνδεές 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 170; μηδὲν ἐνδεὲς λίπῃς [prev. author] “Ph.” 385; ἐνδεὲς φαίνεταί τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.9; ἐνδεές τι ἐν τῷ σώματι ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.40; ἐ. τὸν βίον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 592; τὴν ὄψιν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 1.2}}; τὸ ἐ. **lack, want, defect**, ={ἔνδεια}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77}}; τὸ αὐτῶν ἐ. their **deficiency**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.83.}}
**3** **inferior**, τὰ κρείσσω μηδὲ τἀνδεᾶ λέγειν **the worse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1430; γένει οὐδενὸς ἐνδεής 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.23}}; τῆς δυνάμεως ἐνδεᾶ πρᾶξαι to act **short of** your real power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70}}; τούτου ἐνδεᾶ ἐφαίνετο (i.e. τἃ πράγματα) their power was **unequal to** the purpose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 102: _comparative_ ἐνδεέστερός τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 524; τῆς δόξης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11; αὑτοῦ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 35.}}
**4** **inadequate, insufficient**, πρός τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322b; ἐ. ξυνθῆκαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.36.}}
**5** Grammars, **defective**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 239.18.}}
**6** _adverb_ ἐνδεῶς **defectively, insufficiently**, opposed to ἱκανῶς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 88e, “R.” 523e}}; ἐ. ἔχειν τινός to be **in want of**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 898.8; τῶν ἀναγκαίων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 27}}; μὴ ἐνδεῶς γνῶναι judge not **insufficiently**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40: _comparative_ ἐνδεεστέρως παρεῖχεν ἢ πρὸς τὴν ἐξουσίαν}} **less** than, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.39; ἐ. πρὸς ἃ βούλεται δηλοῦσθαι [prev. author] 2.35; ἐ. ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 74e}}; ἐ. ἢ προσῆκεν τιμωρήσασθαι Epist. Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.12}}: rarely **-έστερον** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 209.21.}}

1731:
** ἐνδείκ-νῡμι**
or **ἐνδεικνύω**, _future_ -δείξω, **mark, point out**, τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.58; πρίν γ᾽ ἂν ἐνδείξω τί δρῶ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 48; ἐ. τῷ δικαστηρίῳ τἀδικήματα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.37, etc.}}; **indicate**, τοὺς καιρούς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.204}}: with _participle_, **show that** a thing is, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 278b; also ἑκάστοις ἐ. τὰ ἔργα ἀποτελεῖν [prev. work] 308e.}}
**2** law-term, **inform against**, τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ap.” 32b: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.20; ἐ. ταῖς ἀρχαῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 856c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.8, etc.; τῷ φήναντι ἢ ἐνδείξαντι “IG” 22.1128.18; ἐ. πρὸς τοὺς μαστῆρας [prev. work]12(7).62.53 (Amorgos, 4th c.BC)}}:— _middle_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 24}}:—frequently in _passive_, κακοῦργος ἐνδεδειγμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.9; ἐνδειχθείς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.15, “OGI” 669.45 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; ἐνδειχθέντα δικάζειν ὀφείλοντα τῷ δημοσίῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.182.}}
**3** **exhibit, display**, ὑπερήφανον αἰχμάν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 406 (Lyric poetry).}}
**4** _middle_, **declare** the possession of goods to fiscal authorities, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PRev.Laws” 54.10 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** _middle_, **show** **forth oneself** or **what is one's own**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, Πηλεΐδῃ ἐνδείξομαι}} I **will declare myself** to Achilles, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.83; ἐνδεικνύμενοι τὴν ἑαυτῶν γνώμην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.141; ἐ. περί τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.28.4; τι μετ᾽ ἀποδείξεως [prev. author] 5.16.7.}}
**II.2** **show, make plain**, with _participle_, πῶς δ᾽ ἂν.. μᾶλλον ἐνδείξαιτό τις πόσιν προτιμῶσ᾽..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 154, compare “Ba.” 47, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.10}}; τὴν δύναμιν κρείττω οὖσαν ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.66; also ἐ. ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.82, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.21; ἐ. ὁποῖα τούτων ἀληθῆ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158e}}:—_passive_, ἐνδεδεῖχθαι τὸ βούλεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.12.}}
**II.2.b** **prove, demonstrate,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PMagd. 3.10 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 11, +others}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **display, exhibit**, τὸ εὔψυχον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126; εὔνοιάν τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 785; τῷ σώματι τὴν εὔνοιαν, οὐ Χρήμας ιν οὐδὲ λόγοις, ἐνεδείξατο τῇ πατρίδι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.145}}; τύπῳ τἀληθὲς ἐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1094b20}}; of a name, **denote**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 394e.}}
**II.4** ἐνδείκνυσθαί τινι **display oneself to** one, **make a set at** him, **court** him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.113, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.217, etc.; ἐνδεικνύμενοι καὶ ὑπερκολακεύοντές τινα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.160}}; **make a show, show off**, τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1352b13.}}

1732:
** ἔνδειξις**, εως, ἡ,
**indication**, ἔνδειξιν τῷ λόγῳ ἐνδείκνυσθαι, opposed to ἐννοεῖν, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 966b, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.38.5, NT.Rom.3.25, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 14.18, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Phas.” p.10 H., 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 62.23, etc.; especially in disease, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.126, +others, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.240.}}
**2** as law-term, **laying of information against** one who discharged public functions for which he was legally disqualified, **writ of indictment** in such a case, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.10, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.156 (_plural_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 52.1 (_plural_), cf. Decrees [prev. work] 29.4, “IG” 22.1128.35.}}
**II** **demonstration, display of one's good will** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (ἐνδείκνυμι 11.4), ἡ εἰς Ἀλέξανδρον ἔ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.219.}}
**III** **proof, demonstration**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteorologicorum librum primum commentarium - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 123.34.}}

1733:
** ἕνδεκα**, οἱ, αἱ, τά,
_indeclinable_, **eleven**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.713, etc.}}
**II** at Athens, οἱ ἕ. **the Eleven, the Police-Commissioners**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1108, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.70, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 7.3, etc.}}
**II.2** certain officers at Delos, {{Refs|“Hermes” 17.5.}}

1735:
** ἐνδέχομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἐν-δέκομαι**, _future_ -ξομαι, **take upon oneself**, ταλαιπωρίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.11.}}
**II** **accept, admit, approve**, τὸν λόγον [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.60; τοὺς λόγους [prev. author] 5.92.á, 96, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 632; τὰ λεγόμενα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82; τὴν συμβουλίην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.51; διαβολάς [prev. author] 3.80; ἀπόστασιν, ={τὸν περὶ ἀποστάσιος λόγον}, [prev. work] 128}}]; so ἐ. [τὴν τοῦ Ἀλκιβιάδου κάθοδον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.50.}}
**II.2** in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} frequently **give ear to, believe**, mostly with a _negative_, ἀρχὴν.. οὐδὲ ἐ. τὸν λόγον {{Refs|5.106; τοῦτο δὲ οὐκ ἐ. ὰρχήν 4.25, compare 3.73, 7.237}}: with _infinitive_, **believe** that.., οὐ γὰρ ἔγωγε ἐ. Ἠριδανόν τινα καλέεσθαι ποταμόν {{Refs|3.115.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, **give ear, attend**, σὺ δ᾽ ἐνδέχου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1238, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 428b}}; περί τινος οὐδ᾽ ὁπωσοῦν ἐ. refuse **to hear a word** about it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.49.}}
**III** of things, **admit, allow of**, τὸ προμηθὲς λογισμὸν οὐκ ἐνδέχεται περί τινος {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.92}}; μεταβολήν, ἀλλοίωσιν ἐ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78d}}; καθ᾽ ὅσον φύσις ἐνδέχεται, **quantum recipit humana condicio**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 69a, compare “Sph.” 254c: with}}_infinitive_, τὸ ναυτικὸν.. οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἐκ παρέργου μελετᾶσθαι **does** not **admit of** being practised, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 9c c, “Lg.” 834d; ὅσων αἱ ἀρχαὶ μὴ ἐνδέχονται ἄλλως ἔχειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1139a7.}}
**III.2** _absolutely_, **to be possible**, ἃ πολλὰ ἐνδέχεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.18}}; ἐὰν ἐνδεχόμενον ᾖ if it be **possible,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PGrenf. 2.14.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; frequently in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, “APr.” 25a38, +others; ἐνδέχεσθαι ἢ εἶναι οὐδὲν διαφέρει ἐν τοῖς ἀιδίοις “Ph.” 203b30; ἐ. μέν, οὐ μὴν ἀναγκαῖον “Pol.” 1275b6}}: especially in _participle_ ἐνδεχόμενος, η, ον, **possible**, ἐκ τῶν ἐνδεχομένων by **all possible means**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.9.4, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.54; αἱ ἐ. τιμωρίαι 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 119; τὴν ἐ. ἀϊδιότητα 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.157b}}; εἰς τὸ ἐ. so **far as possible**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Epitaphius” 41; and frequently in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, τὸ ἐ. ἀληθές “Metaphorical” 1009b34; τῆς ἐ. αὐτοῖς εὐδαιμονίας μετέχειν “Pol.” 1325a10; ζωῆς τῆς ἐ. ἀρίστης [prev. work] 1328a36, +others: frequently with}}_infinitive_, τὰ ἐ. καὶ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι **contingent** events, {{Refs|“GA” 731b25, compare “Metaphorical” 1050b11; τὰ ἐ. ἄλλως ἔχειν “EN” 1134b31, +others; τὰ μὴ ἐ. αὐτῷ πρᾶξαι [prev. work] 1140a32, +others}}
**III.3** ἐνδέχεται _impersonal_, **it admits of** being, **it is possible that**.., with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.124, 140, etc.}}; εἴπερ ἐνεδεχετο (i.e. γράφειν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.239; καθ᾽ ὅσον ἐνδέχεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 271c}}; εἰς ὅσον ἐ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 501c}}; ὅσα ἐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354a32}}; μέχρι οὗ ἐ[{{Refs 4th c.BC+|. [prev. work] 1355b13; ὡς ἐ. μάλιστα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.49.1: _accusative_ _absolutely_, ὥσπερ ἐνδεχόμενον εἶναι, ={ὥσπερ εἰ ἐνδέχοιτο}, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 765b23: _genitive_ _absolutely_, ἐνδεχομένου}}] **where possible**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 683a20.}}
**III.3.b** with _dative_ _person_, **it is allowed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 4.9, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.50.}}

1737:
** ἐνδῐδύσκω**,
**put on**, τινά τι {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Ki.1.24, NT.Mark.15.17}}:—_middle_, **put on oneself,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Luke.8.27, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 7.2.2 (_active_ is variant): written ἐνδυδισκόμενος “SIG” 2857.13 (Delph.).}}
**II** **clothe**, τινὰ ἱματίῳ {{Refs|“Geoponica” 16.21.9.}}

1738:
** ἔνδῐκ-ος**,
{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **ἴνδικος**, ον, (δίκη):
**I** of things, **according to right, just, legitimate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.103}}; γόος ἔ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 330 (Lyric poetry); ὀνείδη [prev. author] “Eu.” 135; λέκτρα “IG” 12(5).675.4 (from Syros)}}; κρίμα [{{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Rom.3.8: τὸ μὴ 'νδικον, ={τὸ ἄδικον}, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 682 (Lyric poetry); τὰ πάντων ἐνδικώτατα [prev. author] “OC” 925}}]; μὴ λέγων γε τοὔνδικον not speaking **truth**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 1158.}}
**I.2** **legal**, ἔ. ἡμέρα a **court**-day, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.25.}}
**I.2.b** **having a locus standi**, μή οἱ ἔστω ἴνδικον μηδέποθι ἀλλ᾽ ἢ.. he shall not have **the right to sue,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(2).6.33 (Tegea, 4th c.BC),compare “Foed.Delph.Pell.” 2 A 16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 915d, “IG” 22.46c56.}}
**I.2.c** ={ἔνοχος δίκα}, {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 3.23, 11.22.}}
**I.2.d** ἔ. πόλις a city **in which justice is done**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 292b}}; **in which sales may be publicly registered,** {{Refs|Milet. Ergebnisse 3.140.}}
**II** of persons, **upright, just**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 699, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 208; πρὸς ἐνδίκοις φρεσίν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 996 (Lyric poetry); δῆμος ἐνδικώτατος [prev. author] “fragment” 196:}}
**II.c** _dative_, ἔ. γάμοις **favourable** to them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 82 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** **possessed of right**, τίς μᾶλλον ἐνδικώτερο; who **has a better right**? {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 673.}}
**III** _adverb_ -κως **right, with justice, fairly**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.” 63, “Ch.” 462 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ὀρθῶς ἐ. τ᾽ ἐπώνυμος [prev. author] “Th.” 405}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 85b.}}
**III.2** **justly, naturally, as one has a right to expect**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 135, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 920.}}

1741:
** ἔνδοξ-ος**, ον,
(δόξα) **held in esteem** or **honour, of high repute**, πρός τινος by one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 6.10 codices (_superlative_)}}; -ότατοι ποιηταί {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.2.56; πόλις -οτέρα εἰς τὰ πολεμικά [prev. work] 3.5.1}}; νέοι πλούσιοι καὶ ἔ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 223b; μὴ πλουσιώτερος ἀλλ᾽ -ότερος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.37; ὀλίγοι καὶ ἔ. ἄνδρες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1098b28, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 7, etc.}}
**2** of things, **notable**, πράγματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.231, compare 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 2.21}}; **generally approved**, τὸ καλόν, ={τὸ ἔ}., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 513}}; **glorious**, ταφαί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 28; ἡδὺ καὶ ἔ. καὶ ὠφέλιμον [prev. author] 2.99f.}} _adverb_ -ξως, frequently in Inscrr., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 442.7 (Erythrae, 3rd c.BC), etc.,compare “Vita Philonidis Epicurei” p.12C., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 1, etc.}}: _comparative_ -οτέρως, τὰ ἔνδοξα ἐ. λέγειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.9}}; also στῆλαι ἔχουσαι ἐπιγραφὰς -ξως **conspicuously** placed, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 6152.22 (1st c.BC)}}: _superlative_ -ότατα, ἐβουλεύσασθε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.65.}}
**II** **resting on opinion, probable, generally admitted**, ἔ. τὰ δοκοῦντα πᾶσιν ἢ τοῖς πλείστοις ἢ τοῖς σοφοῖς, opposed to to what is necessarily true (τὰ πρῶτα καὶ ἀληθῆ), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 100b21, compare “EN” 1145b5, “Rh.” 1355a17, +others}}; ἡ ἔ. διδασκαλία **popular** teaching, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.247.}}
**II.2** _adverb_ -ξως, συλλογίζεσθαι **plausibly**, opposed to ἀληθῶς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 175a31.}}
**III** **conceited**, οὐκ ἔνδοξοι πρὸς τὸ μαθεῖν ἃ μὴ ἴσμεν not **too proud** to learn, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus “fragment” 60.}}

1743:
** ἐνδυνᾰμ-όω**,
**strengthen, confirm**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Josh.6.34, NT.1Tim.1.12, +others}}:—_passive_, ἐπὶ τῇ ματαιότητι {{LXX|LXX.Psa.51(52).9}}; τῇ πίστει {{NT|NT.Rom.4.20, +others}}
**II** **endow with vitality,** in _passive_, _metaphorically_ of scientific theorems, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.9.5.}}

1744:
** ἐνδύω**
or **ἐνδύνω** (ἐνδυνέω variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.98)}}, with _middle_ **ἐνδύομαι**, _future_ -δύσομαι: _aorist 1_ -εδυσάμη; _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ or _imperfect_ -εδυσόμην: _aorist 2_ _active_ -έδυν: _perfect_ -δέδῡκα:
**I** with _accusative_ _of things_ vel loci, **go into,**
**I.1** of clothes, **put on,** ἔνδυνε χιτῶνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.42; ἔνδυνε περὶ στήθεσσι χιτῶνα 10.21; χιτῶν᾽ ἐνδῦσα 5.736; τιὡς θώρηκα ἐνδύνουσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.98; ἐνδύντες τὰ ὅπλα [prev. author] 1.172}}; τὴν σκευήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 24; πέπλον ἐνδύς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 759, etc.:}}_perfect_ ἐνδέδῡκα, **wear,** κιθῶνας λινέους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.81, compare 7.64, 9.22; λεοντῆν ἐνδέδυκα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 411a}}:—_middle_, ἐν δ᾽ αὐτὸς ἐδύσετο χαλκόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.578, 11.16}}; ἐνδύεσθαι ὅπλα variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.218; σκευάς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130; ἐνδύσεται στολήν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 853: _metaphorically_, ἐνδυόμενοι τόλμημα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 288}}; also τὸν Ταρκύνιον ἐνδύεσθαι **assume** the person of T., {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.5; τὸν καινὸν ἄνθρωπον NT.Eph.4.24}}:—_passive_, **to be clothed in, have on,** ἐσθῆτα ἐνδεδύσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Insomn.” 91, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 432.}}
**I.2** **enter, press into,** with _accusative_, ἐν δέ οἱ ἦτορ δῦν᾽ ἄχος ἄτλητον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.367}}; ἀκοντιστὺν ἐνδύσεαι thou **wilt enter** the contest {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus ἐσδύσεαι), 23.622; τὴν τοῦ Θερσίτου [ψυχὴν] πίθηκον ἐνδυομένην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 620c; εὔνοια ἐνδύεταί τινα [prev. author] “Lg.” 642b; ἔρως δεινὸς ἐνδέδυκέ τινος [prev. author] “Tht.” 169c}}; also ἐ. εἰς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1020, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 609b21}}; εἰς τὴν ἐπιμέλειαν ἐνδῦναι **enter upon** it, **undertake** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.12}}: _absolutely_, **enter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89d: with _dative_, ἐ. ταῖς ψυχαῖς τῶν ἀκουόντων}} **insinuate oneself into** their minds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.13; τοῖς ταύροις τὸν οἶστρον ἐνδύεσθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.55e, etc.}}; ἐνὶ χροῒ δύετο ῥινὸς ἐντυπάς Epic.in{{Refs|“Arch.Papyrus” 7.3:}}_perfect_ _passive_, φυσικαῖς ἐνδεδυμένος αἰτίαις uncertain in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.435f (to be read -δεδεμένος)}}: _absolutely_, **creep in,** variant for{ἐσ-}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. β; ἐ. διά τινος}}**slip through,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.38a, etc.}}
**I.3** **sink in,** hence τρίβος ἐνδεδυκώς **sunken** path, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Arat.” 22}}; ῥίς **sunken** nose, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Publ.” 16.}}
**II** causal in _present_ ἐνδύω, _future_ -δύσω: _aorist 1_ -έδυσα:—**put on** another, **clothe in,** with _double_ _accusative_, τὴν ἐξωμίδ᾽ ἐνδύσω σε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1021; ὃς ἐμὲ κροκόεντ᾽ ἐνέδυσεν [prev. author] “Th.” 1044, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.3.}}
**II.2** **clothe,** ἐνδύουσι τὤγαλμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.42; ἐὰν.. πένητα γυμνὸν ἐνδύσῃς 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 176}}; σύ με ἐνδέδυκας [probably ῠ] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 77.8 (2nd c.AD).}}

1745:
** ἔνδῠσις**, εως, ἡ,
(ἐνδύω) **entry,** coined by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 419c; [σελήνη] ἐν Κρόνου ἐνδύσει 6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus 12.}}
**2** ={κατάδυσις}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** **putting on,** ἱματίων {{NT|NT.1Pet.3.3}}; **dressing, dress,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Est.5.1, Aristeas 96, 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 57.}}

1746:
** ἐνδύω**
or **ἐνδύνω** (ἐνδυνέω variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.98)}}, with _middle_ **ἐνδύομαι**, _future_ -δύσομαι: _aorist 1_ -εδυσάμη; _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ or _imperfect_ -εδυσόμην: _aorist 2_ _active_ -έδυν: _perfect_ -δέδῡκα:
**I** with _accusative_ _of things_ vel loci, **go into,**
**I.1** of clothes, **put on,** ἔνδυνε χιτῶνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.42; ἔνδυνε περὶ στήθεσσι χιτῶνα 10.21; χιτῶν᾽ ἐνδῦσα 5.736; τιὡς θώρηκα ἐνδύνουσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.98; ἐνδύντες τὰ ὅπλα [prev. author] 1.172}}; τὴν σκευήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 24; πέπλον ἐνδύς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 759, etc.:}}_perfect_ ἐνδέδῡκα, **wear,** κιθῶνας λινέους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.81, compare 7.64, 9.22; λεοντῆν ἐνδέδυκα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 411a}}:—_middle_, ἐν δ᾽ αὐτὸς ἐδύσετο χαλκόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.578, 11.16}}; ἐνδύεσθαι ὅπλα variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.218; σκευάς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130; ἐνδύσεται στολήν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 853: _metaphorically_, ἐνδυόμενοι τόλμημα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 288}}; also τὸν Ταρκύνιον ἐνδύεσθαι **assume** the person of T., {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.5; τὸν καινὸν ἄνθρωπον NT.Eph.4.24}}:—_passive_, **to be clothed in, have on,** ἐσθῆτα ἐνδεδύσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Insomn.” 91, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 432.}}
**I.2** **enter, press into,** with _accusative_, ἐν δέ οἱ ἦτορ δῦν᾽ ἄχος ἄτλητον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.367}}; ἀκοντιστὺν ἐνδύσεαι thou **wilt enter** the contest {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus ἐσδύσεαι), 23.622; τὴν τοῦ Θερσίτου [ψυχὴν] πίθηκον ἐνδυομένην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 620c; εὔνοια ἐνδύεταί τινα [prev. author] “Lg.” 642b; ἔρως δεινὸς ἐνδέδυκέ τινος [prev. author] “Tht.” 169c}}; also ἐ. εἰς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1020, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 609b21}}; εἰς τὴν ἐπιμέλειαν ἐνδῦναι **enter upon** it, **undertake** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.12}}: _absolutely_, **enter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89d: with _dative_, ἐ. ταῖς ψυχαῖς τῶν ἀκουόντων}} **insinuate oneself into** their minds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.13; τοῖς ταύροις τὸν οἶστρον ἐνδύεσθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.55e, etc.}}; ἐνὶ χροῒ δύετο ῥινὸς ἐντυπάς Epic.in{{Refs|“Arch.Papyrus” 7.3:}}_perfect_ _passive_, φυσικαῖς ἐνδεδυμένος αἰτίαις uncertain in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.435f (to be read -δεδεμένος)}}: _absolutely_, **creep in,** variant for{ἐσ-}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. β; ἐ. διά τινος}}**slip through,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.38a, etc.}}
**I.3** **sink in,** hence τρίβος ἐνδεδυκώς **sunken** path, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Arat.” 22}}; ῥίς **sunken** nose, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Publ.” 16.}}
**II** causal in _present_ ἐνδύω, _future_ -δύσω: _aorist 1_ -έδυσα:—**put on** another, **clothe in,** with _double_ _accusative_, τὴν ἐξωμίδ᾽ ἐνδύσω σε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1021; ὃς ἐμὲ κροκόεντ᾽ ἐνέδυσεν [prev. author] “Th.” 1044, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.3.}}
**II.2** **clothe,** ἐνδύουσι τὤγαλμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.42; ἐὰν.. πένητα γυμνὸν ἐνδύσῃς 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 176}}; σύ με ἐνδέδυκας [probably ῠ] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 77.8 (2nd c.AD).}}

1747:
** ἐνέδρ-α**, ἡ,
**sitting in**: hence, **lying in wait, ambush,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.56 (_plural_), etc.; ἐ. ποιεῖσθαι [prev. author] 3.90; ἐνέδραι κατασκευάζονται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 4.10; ἐνέδραν τιθέναι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.108; θέσθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 23; εἰς ἐ. ἐμπίπτειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.14}}; ἐκ τῆς ἐ. ἀνίστασθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 5.4.4; θέειν ἐκ τῆς ἐ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.67.}}
**b** **men laid in ambush,** τὴν ἐ. ἐζανιστάναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.37.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **trickery, treachery,** δόλου καὶ ἐνέδρας πλήρης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 908d, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.77; ἐνέδρας ἕνεκα 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 124.7}}; ἐξ ἐνέδρας, opposed to φανερῶς, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.422; μετ᾽ ἐνέδρας 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.30, cf. Archig.ap 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.2.20.}}
**II** **position,** ναρθήκων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 16, 27.}}
**III** **delay,** περί τι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 62.10 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}

1748:
** ἐνεδρ-εύω**,
_imperfect_ ἐνήδρευον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.39}}: _future_ ἐνεδρεύσω {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 63}}: _aorist_ ἐνήδρευσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.67, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.22, etc.:}} — _middle_, _future_ -σομαι (in _passive_ sense) {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 7.2.18}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐνηδρεύθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.2}}: _perfect_ ἐνήδρευμαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cal.” 23}}: (ἐνέδρα):—**lie in wait for, lay snares for,** τινά {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.10, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Κόλαξ” 44}}: _passive_, **to be caught in an ambush, to be ensnared,** of animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.5; μέλιτι 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 16}}; of persons to whom poison has been given, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 10 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus: _metaphorically_, ὑπὸ νόμων τοὺς πολίτας ἐνεδρεύεσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.49}}; εἰ.. μὴ τῷ χρόνῳ ἐνηδρεύθημεν if we **had** not **been deceived** by time, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.2.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **lay** or **set an ambush,** ἐς τὸ Ἐνυάλιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.67, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.2, 4.1.22,etc.}}
**II** **place in ambush,** πεζούς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.76, variant in 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 5.8.11}}:—_middle_, _absolutely_, **set an ambush,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.15}}:—_passive_, _metaphorically_, οἱ ἐνηδρευμένοι τῇ δημηγορίᾳ λόγοι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.17.}}
**III** **hinder, obstruct,** τινάς {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1773.33 (3rd c.AD)}}; διάπρασιν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“PGiss.” 105.24 (5th c.AD)}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.143.9 (4th c.AD).}}

1750:
** ἐνειλ-έω**,
**wrap in,** τι ὀθονίῳ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.72}}:—_middle_, τινὰ κακοῖσι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 14.294}}:—_passive_, **to be enwrapped,** ἐν [τῇ γῇ] {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 396a14; ἐν τῷ ἱματίῳ LXX.1Ki.21.9(10); τῃ λεοντῇ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 12a. 1; ῥάκεσι 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.13; ἱστίοις δοράτια ἐνειλημένα 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 29.6, compare 31.7; φύλλοις 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.80.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **engage,** ἐνίων αὑτοὺς ἐνειληκότων οἰκονομίαις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 24.62 (2nd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, **to be engaged, entangled in** or **with,** τοῖς πολεμίοις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 11; ὅπλοις [prev. author] “Brut.” 45; ὥσπερ θηρίον ταῖς πάντων χερσίν [prev. author] “Caes.” 66}}; ὥσπερ ἄρκυσιν ἐνειλημένους probably to be read instead of -λημμ-, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.2.8}}; βρέφη-ημένα τὰς χεῖρας Artem.[same place]; **come to blows with,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PRyl. 144.18 (1st c.AD).}}

1751:
** ἔνειμι**
(εἰμί, **sum**), _3rd.pers._ _singular_ and _plural_ ἔνι frequently for ἔνεστι, ἔνεισι (see. below): _infinitive_ ἐνεῖμεν {{Refs|“IG” 22.1126.24 (Amphict.Delph.): _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἔνι frequently for}}_future_ ἐνέσομαι:—**to be in,** ἄργυρος ἀσκῷ ἔνεστι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.45}}; ἔνι (for ἔνεστι) κήδεα θυμῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.53; ἔνι τοι φρένες οὐδ᾽ ἠβαιαί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.288; εἰ.. χάλκεον.. μοι ἦτορ ἐνείη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.490}}; εἴ τι ἐνέοι (i.e. τοῖς χρησμοῖσι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6; νοῦς ἔνεστιν ὑμῖν ἐγγενής 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1328}}; τοῖς λόγοις ἔ. κέρδος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 370; πόλλ᾽ ἔ. τῷ γήρᾳ κακά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 441; πλήθη, ἐν οἷς τὸ ἓν οὐκ ἔνι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 158c; στάσιν ἐνέσεσθαι τῇ γνώμῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.20; εἴσοι πυκνότης ἔνεστ᾽ ἐν τῷ τρόπῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1132; ἐνῆν ἄρ᾽.. κἀν οἴνῳ λόγος 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 41; ἀγαθὸς βαφεὺς ἔνεστιν ἐν τῷ παιδιῳ 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 72: ἔνι τις καὶ ἐν ἡμῖν παῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 77e; also ἐν τοῖσιν οὔρεσι δένδρεα ἔνι ἄγρια 4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 292 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; ἐν [ὄρει] ἔνι μέταλλα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.112; ἐν τῷ προθυμεῖσθαι ἐνοῦσαν ζημίαν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 383, etc.}}
**b** with _dative_ _plural_, **to be among,** {{NT+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1135, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81, +others; οὐκ ἔνι ἐν ὑμῖν οὐδεὶς σοφός NT.1Cor.6.5.}}
**c** with _adverb_ loci, οἴκοι ἔνεστι γόος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.240}}; ἔνεστιν αὐτόθι **is in** this very place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 119; ἐνταῦθα [prev. author] “Nu.” 211, etc.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **to be present in** a place, οἶνος ἐνέην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.164}}; οὐδ᾽ ἄνδρες νηῶν ἔνι τέκτονες{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 126; οὐδ᾽ ἔνι στάσις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 738 (troch.); Ἄρης οὐκ ἔνι χώρᾳ [prev. author] “Ag.” 78 (_anapaest meter_)}}; σίτου οὐκ ἐνόντος **as there was** no corn **there,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8}}; τὰ ἐνόντα ἀγαθά the good **that is therein,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 20}}; ἱερῶν τῶν ἐνόντων the temples **that were in the place,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 97; ἀμέλειά τις ἐνῆν καὶ διατριβή [prev. author] 5.38; πόλεμος οὐκ ἐνῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271e; μηδὲ μύλαν ἐνεῖμεν μηδὲ ὅλμον “IG” 22.[same place]}}; also, **to be mentioned in** a treaty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.43, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 974}}; χρόνος ἐνέσται time **will be necessary,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80}}; ἡ βὴξ ἔνι the cough **is persistent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.12.}}
**II** **to be possible,** ἄρνησις οὐκ ἔ. ὧν ἀνιστορεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 578; τῶνδ᾽ ἄρνησις οὐκ ἔ. μοι [prev. author] “El.” 527}}; τίς δ᾽ ἔνεστί μοι λόγο; what plea **is possible** for me [to make]? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 998; οὐκ ἐνῆν πρόφασις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.25; οὐκ ἐνέσται αὐτῷ λόγος οὐδὲ εἷς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.41; εἴ τι ἄλλο ἐνῆν [prev. author] 18.190; ἐνούσης οὐδεμιᾶς ἔτ᾽ ἀποστροφῆς [prev. author] 24.9.}}
**II.2** _impersonal_, with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **it is in one's power,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 296, “Ant.” 213, etc.}}: with _infinitive_ only, οὔκουν ἔ. καὶ μεταγνῶνα; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1270; οὐ γὰρ δὴ τοῦτό γ᾽ ἔνεστιν εἰπεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.14; πῶς ἔ. ἢ πῶς δυνατό; [prev. author] 57.24, etc.}}; οὐκ ἔνεστι **it is** not **possible,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 22.7}}; ὃ μὴ νεώς γε τῆς ἐμῆς ἔνι which **it is** not **possible** [to get] from my ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 648 (sed to be read ἔπι): ἔνι is frequently in this sense, ἃ δὲ ἔνι [λέγειν] 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.4; δι᾽ ὀργήν γ᾽ ἔνι φῆσαι πεποιηκέναι [prev. author] 21.41}}; ὡς ἔνι ἥδιστα in the pleasantest way **possible,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.5.9, compare 3.8.4; ὡς ἔνι μάλιστα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.4.14, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.465, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.” 6, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.218c}}: _imperfect_, ὡς ἐνῆν ἄριστα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tyr.” 17.}}
**II.2.b** ἔνεστιν ὑμᾶς εἰδέναι **it is relevant, pertinent**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 486.12 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.3** _participle_ ἐνόν, _absolutely_, ἐνὸν αὐτοῖς σώζεσθαι **since it was in** them, **was possible for** them, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.3.2, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 9.}}
**II.4** τὰ ἐνόντα **all things possible**: τὸ πλῆθος τῶν ἐ. εἰπεῖν the **possible materials** for a speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.110, compare 11.44; τῶν ἐ... ἐν τῷ πράγματι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 235b; τῶν φαινομένων καὶ ἐ. τὰ κράτιστα ἑλέσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.190}}; ἐκ τῶν ἐ. **as well as one can under the circumstances,** {{Refs|[prev. work]256}}; τὰ ἐ. καὶ τὰ ἁρμόττοντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1450b5: in _sing._, πᾶν τὸ ἐνὸν ἐκλέγων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.59.}}
**II.4.b** τὰ ἐνόντα **cargo** or **stores** in a ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 488c}}; **contents** of a basket, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 414.20 (2nd c.AD).}}

1752:
** ἕνεκα**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.110, etc.}}, or **ἕνεκεν** (twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.288, 310, rare in Trag., as 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 999 (Lyric poetry), and early Prose, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 210e; in Comedy texts, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 330}}; twice in 4th C _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 2.987A2, 611b13, but prevalent in later Inscrr., compare “SIG” 577.7 (Milet., 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; in late Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.10)}}, _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, and _poetry_ **εἵνεκα** (also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, “Lg.” 778d, +others)}}, or **εἵνεκεν** (both forms in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus and 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}} and not uncommon in codices of later writers; εἵνεκεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.136, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).35 codices}}; **εἵνεκε** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.2, _falsa lectio_ in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.133)}}: **ἕνεκε** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 333.14 (Samos, 4th c.BC), “Supp.epigram” 1.351.10 ([prev. passage]), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3655.18 (Cyzicus, 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}: _Aeolic dialect_ **ἔννεκα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 9.1, “IG” 12(2).258.8 (Lesbos, 1st c.AD)}}, but **ἔνεκα** {{Refs|[prev. work] 11(4).1064b32 (from Delos), 12(1).645a38 (from Nesus): late}}**ἕνεκον** {{Refs|“JHS” 37.108 (from Lydia)}}, etc.:—_preposition_ with _genitive_, usually after its case; also before, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.94, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.136, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.122, etc.}} When it follows its case, it is sometimes separated from it by several words, as in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.88, etc.}}
**1** **on account of,** Τρώων πόλιν.. ἧς εἵνεκ᾽ ὀϊζύομεν κακὰ πολλά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.89, etc.; ὕβριος εἵνεκα τῆσδε 1.214}}; τοῦδ᾽ ἕνεκα **for** this,{{Refs|[prev. work] 110; ὧν ἕ.}}**wherefore,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|20.21; τίνος ἕ. βλάβη; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 181; παῖσαι ἄνδρας ἕνεκεν ἀταξίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.13; στεφανοῦσθαι ἀρετῆς ἕνεκα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.10}}; **for the sake of,** τοῦ ἕ.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 31c b}}; τῶν δὲ εἵνεκα, ὅκως.., or ἵνα.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.35, 40; κολακεύειν ἕ. μισθοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.17}}; διὰ νόσον ἕ. ὑγιείας by reason of sickness **for the sake of** health, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 218e, compare “Smp.” 185b}}; τὸ οὗ ἕ. the **final cause,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 194a27, “Metaphorical” 983a31; τὸ οὗ ἕνεκεν [prev. author] “Ph.” 243a3, “Metaphorical” 1059a35.}}
**2** **as far as regards,** ἐμοῦ γ᾽ ἕνεκα **as far as depends on** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 386, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.14; τοῦ φυλάσσοντος εἵνεκεν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.42}}; εἵνεκεν χρημάτων **as for** money, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.122, etc.; ἕνεκά γε φιλονικίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 548d, compare 329b}}; ἐμπειρίας μὲν ἄρα ἕ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 582d; ὁμοῖοι τοῖς τυφλοῖς ἂν ἦμεν ἕνεκά γε τῶν ἡμετέρων ὀφθαλμῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.3.}}
**3** **in consequence of,** εἵνεκα τέχνας **by force of** art, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.729.}}
**4** _pleonastic_, ἀμφὶσοὔνεκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 554 codices}}; ὅσον ἀπὸ βοῆς ἕ. **as far as** shouting **went,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.31; τίνος χάριν ἕ.; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 701d, compare “Plt.” 302b.}}
**II** _conjunction_, for οὕνεκα (which see), **because,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|hymnus ad Venerem 199, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.6, “fragment” 287.}}
**II.2** εἵνεκεν, ={ὁθούνεκα}, **that,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).35 codices}}

1753:
** ἐνέργ-εια**, ἡ,
**activity, operation,** opposed to ἕξις (disposition), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1098b33, +others; ζῴου 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.4.7; ἡ χαρὰ καὶ ἡ εὐφροσύνη κατὰ κίνησιν ἐνεργείᾳ βλέπονται 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 2}}; opposed to ἀογία, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 19p.461M.: _plural_, παντοδαπαὶ ἐ. Polystr. p.30 W.; ἐ. καὶ σπουδή “PTeb.” 616 (2nd c.AD)}}; physiological **function,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.21}}; **performance,** τῶν καθηκόντων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.91}}; **activity,** of drugs, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.467}}; **force,** of an engine, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.95}} (but, **mechanism, 'action',** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 1.7).}}
**b** **workmanship,** Aristeas {{Refs|59.}}
**2** especially of divine or supernatural **action,** {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Eph.1.19, +others, Aristeas 266; ἐ. θεοῦ Διὸς Βαιτοκαίκης “OGI” 262.4 (Syria, 3rd c.AD)}}; magical **operation,** ἱερὰ ἐ. {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.159.}}
**3** _plural_, ἐνέργειαι **cosmic forces,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.41.6.}}
**4** Grammars, **active force,** opposed to πάθος, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 637.29, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 9.9 (_plural_), +others}}; ἐνέργειαι καὶ πάθη **active** and passive **forms,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor “De Figuris” 2.14.}}
**5** Rhet., **vigour of style,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1411b28.}}
**II** in the philosophical of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, opposed to δύναμις}} **actuality, Metaphorical**{{Refs|1048a26, +others}}; opposed to ὕλη, {{Refs|[prev. work]1043a20}}; ἡ ὡς ἐ. οὐσία, substance in the sense of **actuality,** {{Refs|[prev. work]1042b10}}; opposed to ἐντελέχεια, as **actuality** to **full reality,** {{Refs|[prev. work]1050a22, 1047a30}}; ἐνεργείᾳ **actually,** opposed to δυνάμει, {{Refs|[prev. work]1045b19, +others, etc.}}

1754:
** ἐνεργ-έω**,
**to be in action** or **activity, operate,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1411b26; εὐδαίμων ὁ κατ᾽ ἀρετὴν τελείαν -ῶν [prev. author] “EN” 1101a15: with _accusative_, ἐνεργεῖς ποσὸν καὶ δυάδα 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.6.16}}; especially of divine or supernatural **action,** frequently in **NT.,** τινί, ἔν τινι, {{NT|NT.Gal.2.8, NT.Matt.14.2}}:—_middle_, {{NT|NT.Rom.7.5, +others}}
**2** **to be efficacious,** of drugs, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Diocl.fragment 147, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.98, +others; ἐοικότα ἐ. [prev. work]106; ἐνήργησε τὸ φάρμακον 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.1.22}}; **to be effective,** of troops, ταῖς σαρίσαις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 14.6}}:—_passive_, **to be the object of action,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 427a7, “Ph.” 195b28; ὁ ἐνεργούμενος λίθος “IG” 7.3073.108 (Lebad.)}}; also, **to be actualized,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.7.11.}}
**II** _transitive_, **effect, execute,** πάντα κατὰ δύναμιν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.14.8; τὰ τοῦ πολέμου [prev. author] 7.5.8; χρείαν 2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus 14.2, compare 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus 2.9}}:—_passive_, **to be actively carried on,** ὁ πόλεμος ἐνηργεῖτο {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.13.5, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.95; τὰ κατὰ πρόθεσιν ἐνεργούμενα}}**things executed,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.12.7; μηδὲν ἐνέργημα.. ἐνεργείσθω 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 4.2.}}
**III** in Medicine texts, of sexual intercourse, τὴν τοιαύτην πρᾶξιν -οῦσι μετρίως {{Refs|Diocl.fragment 141, compare “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).176: euphemistic for βινεῖν}} **in opere esse,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.61; ἐ. τινά 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.55; ἐρωτικόν τι [prev. author] 1.39.}}
**IV** **operate,** in surgery, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.18.5, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.73}}; ὁ ἐνεργῶν, ὁ ἐνεργούμενος, the **surgeon,** the **patient,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).626,683.}}

1755:
** ἐνέργ-ημα**, ατος, τό,
**action, activity, operation,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.8.7, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.51 (of the labours of Heracles), 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.213, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 4.2, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 158, +others: _plural_, φύσεων 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 4.13}}; opposed to πάθος, {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.59, compare 3.134, Chrysipp.[prev. work]2.295.}}
**2** **realized object,** [νοῦς] αὑτοῦ ἐ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.8.16, compare 6.9.2.}}
**3** uncertain for ἐνάργημα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.4U.; τὸ κατὰ φιλοσοφίαν ἐ. 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.8, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.68.}}

1758:
** ἐνέχω**,
**hold** or **keep fast within,** χόλον ἐνέχειν τινί **harbour** a grudge against one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.118, 6.119 (see. 11.2).}}
**II** _passive_, with _future_ and _aorist_ _middle_ (see. below), **to be held, caught, entangled in,** with _dative_, τῇ πάγῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.121. ; ἐνεχομένων τῶν πελτῶν τοῖς σταυροῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.17; ἐν τοῖς τῆς νεὼς σκεύεσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 183e.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, ἐ. ἀπορίῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.190; φιλοτιμίᾳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 527; ὀργαῖς πολυχρονίοις 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.63 W.; ἐν ἄγεϊ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.56; ἐν ταῖς αὐταῖς δυσχερείαις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 309a29}}; ἐν θώματι ἐνέσχετο **was seized with** wonder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.128.}}
**II.3** **to be liable** or **subject to,** οὐ δικαίοις Ζεὺς ἐνέξεται λόγοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 169 (Pors. for ἐνεύξεται, Lyric poetry),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.44}}; πράγμασιν, λειτουργίαις, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 473.7 (2nd c.AD), “PFlor.” 382.31 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**II.3.b** in legal formulae, ἐν ἐπιάρῳ κ᾽ ἐνέχοιτο {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9.9 (Elis, 6th c.BC)}}; ἐ. ἀρᾷ Διός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 881d (in tmesi, ἐν τἠπαρῇ ἔχεσθαι “SIG” 38.34 (Teos, 5th c.BC))}}; ζημίᾳ, αἰτίᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 935c, “Cri.” 52a; τοῖς ἐσχάτοις ἐπιτιμίοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 51.11; ἐν τοῖς αὐτοῖς ἐπιτιμίοις 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.175; νόμῳ “Schwyzer” 634 B 49 (Nesus, 4th c.BC, probably), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 10; ἐν τοῖς αὐτοῖς νόμοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 762d}}; νοθείᾳ **in an imputation of** bastardy, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 1; ἱεροσυλίαις “PTeb.” 5.5 (2nd c.BC): _absolutely_, ἐὰν ἐνσχεθῶσι “PSI” 3.168 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.4** in good sense, ἐνέχεσθαι ἀγγελίᾳ **meet with** a message, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.49.}}
**II.5** in _aorist_, **come to a standstill,** ἔν τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147d.}}
**III** _intransitive_, **enter in, pierce,** εἴς τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.7.}}
**III.2** **to be urgent against,** τινί {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.49.23, NT.Mark.6.19, NT.Luke.11.53.}}

1759:
** ἐνθάδε** [ᾰ],
_adverb_:
**I** of Place, **thither, hither,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.492, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 304, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.36, etc.}}
**I.2** after [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus more frequently, ={ἔνθα}}}], **here** or **there,** ἐνθάδε αὐτοῦ μένων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 765}}; **in this world,** opposed to the nether-world, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.57, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 525b; ὁ δ᾽ εὔκολος μὲν ἐνθάδ᾽ εὔκολος δ᾽ ἐκεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 82}}; οἱ ἐ., opposed to οἱ κάτω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 923, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 75}}; also, the people **of this country,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 42}}; τοῖς ἐνθάδ᾽ αὐτοῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 78; τις τῶν ἐνθάδ᾽ αὐτοῦ 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 357}}; τὰ ἐνθάδε, opposed to τὰ ἐκεῖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.17.}}
**II** of circumstances, **in this case** or **state,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.17}}; ἐνθάδ᾽ ἥκων having come **to this point,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 377: with _genitive_, ἐ. τοῦ πάθους}} **at this stage** of my suffering, {{Refs|[prev. work] 899.}}
**II.2** of Time, **here, now,** οὔτ᾽ ἐνθάδ᾽ ὁρῶν οὔτ᾽ ὀπίσω neither **the present** nor the future, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 488 (Lyric poetry); αὐτίκ᾽ ἐ. [prev. author] “OC” 992.}}

1760:
** ἐνθῡμ-έομαι**,
_future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.45}}, later **-ηθήσομαι** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.26.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 21, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐνεθυμήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 40, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.62, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.27, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἐντεθύμημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120}}: _pluperfect_ ἐνετεθύμητο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.70}}:—**lay to heart, ponder,** ἤτοι κρίνομέν γε ἢ ἐνθυμούμεθα ὀρθῶς τὰ πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40; ἄξιον ἐνθυμηθῆναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.20; πρὸς ἐμαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.50}}; ἐ. καὶ λογίζεσθαι frequently joined in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, as 1.21, +others}}
**b** with _genitive_, ἐνθυμεῖσθαί τινος **think much** or **deeply of,** τοῦ θανόντος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 2; τούτων οὐδὲν ἐ. 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 41; τῶν λεγομένων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.6; ὧν ἐνθυμηθέντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.42, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 249c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.17; τῶν προγόνων ἐ. ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.20; also περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 595a.}}
**c** followed by a _relative_, ἐ. ὅτι.. **notice** or **consider** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 820, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.111, etc.}}; ὡς.. how.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 40, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.3, etc.; εἰ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.60; μὴ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 279c, “Hp.Ma.” 300d.}}
**d** with _participle_, οὐκ ἐντεθύμηται ἐπαιρόμενος **is** not **conscious** that he is becoming excited, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120, compare 6.78, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.19.}}
**2** **take to heart, be concerned** or **angry at,** τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 222; ξυμφοράν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.18, compare 5.32 (see. ἐνθυμίζομαι); εἰ μηδεὶς ὑμῶν μήτ᾽ ἐνθυμεῖται μήτ᾽ ὀργίζεται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.43}}: _absolutely_, **to be concerned,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 22; ={ἐνθύμιον ποιεῖσθαι}, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 57.4.}}]
**3** **form a plan,** κράτιστος ἐνθυμηθῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68, compare 2.60}}; **take care, see to it,** ἐ. ἵνα μηθεὶς ἀδικῇ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.436.9 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4** **infer, conclude,** τί οὖν ἐκ τούτων.. ἐνθυμεῖσθαι δε; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.54.}}
**II** _active_, ἐνθυμέω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 99.4, 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 37.6 (assuming variant)}}; ἐνθυμέομαι, in _passive_ sense, **to be in a person's thoughts, to be desired,** κρατεῖν τῶν ἐνθυμουμένων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.133}}: _perfect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(compare 1.3), ταυτὶ μὲν ἡμῖν ἐντεθύμηται καλῶς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 262; εὖ ἐντεθυμημένον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 404a (nisi to be read φιλοσόφου.. καὶ εὖ ἐντεθυμημένου).}}

1761:
** ἐνθῡμ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**consideration, esteem,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 246.}}
**II** **consideration, reflection,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.132, NT.Matt.9.4 (_plural_), 2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Job.21.27, Vett. Val.301.8, etc.}}
**II.2** **idea, conception,** εἰς ἐ. ἐσθήτων ἦλθον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus Oen.10; τὰς ἐ. ὀξύς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 81.}}
**III** **anxiety, worry,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 4.}}
**IV** **resolution**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“BGU” 1024 iv 12 (4th c.AD).}}

1762:
** ἔνειμι**
(εἰμί, **sum**), _3rd.pers._ _singular_ and _plural_ **ἔνι **frequently for ἔνεστι, ἔνεισι (see. below): _infinitive_ ἐνεῖμεν {{Refs|“IG” 22.1126.24 (Amphict.Delph.): _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἔνι frequently for}}_future_ ἐνέσομαι:—**to be in,** ἄργυρος ἀσκῷ ἔνεστι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.45}}; ἔνι (for ἔνεστι) κήδεα θυμῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.53; ἔνι τοι φρένες οὐδ᾽ ἠβαιαί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.288; εἰ.. χάλκεον.. μοι ἦτορ ἐνείη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.490}}; εἴ τι ἐνέοι (i.e. τοῖς χρησμοῖσι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6; νοῦς ἔνεστιν ὑμῖν ἐγγενής 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1328}}; τοῖς λόγοις ἔ. κέρδος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 370; πόλλ᾽ ἔ. τῷ γήρᾳ κακά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 441; πλήθη, ἐν οἷς τὸ ἓν οὐκ ἔνι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 158c; στάσιν ἐνέσεσθαι τῇ γνώμῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.20; εἴσοι πυκνότης ἔνεστ᾽ ἐν τῷ τρόπῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1132; ἐνῆν ἄρ᾽.. κἀν οἴνῳ λόγος 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 41; ἀγαθὸς βαφεὺς ἔνεστιν ἐν τῷ παιδιῳ 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 72: ἔνι τις καὶ ἐν ἡμῖν παῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 77e; also ἐν τοῖσιν οὔρεσι δένδρεα ἔνι ἄγρια 4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 292 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; ἐν [ὄρει] ἔνι μέταλλα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.112; ἐν τῷ προθυμεῖσθαι ἐνοῦσαν ζημίαν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 383, etc.}}
**b** with _dative_ _plural_, **to be among,** {{NT+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1135, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81, +others; οὐκ ἔνι ἐν ὑμῖν οὐδεὶς σοφός NT.1Cor.6.5.}}
**c** with _adverb_ loci, οἴκοι ἔνεστι γόος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.240}}; ἔνεστιν αὐτόθι **is in** this very place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 119; ἐνταῦθα [prev. author] “Nu.” 211, etc.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **to be present in** a place, οἶνος ἐνέην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.164}}; οὐδ᾽ ἄνδρες νηῶν ἔνι τέκτονες{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 126; οὐδ᾽ ἔνι στάσις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 738 (troch.); Ἄρης οὐκ ἔνι χώρᾳ [prev. author] “Ag.” 78 (_anapaest meter_)}}; σίτου οὐκ ἐνόντος **as there was** no corn **there,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8}}; τὰ ἐνόντα ἀγαθά the good **that is therein,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 20}}; ἱερῶν τῶν ἐνόντων the temples **that were in the place,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 97; ἀμέλειά τις ἐνῆν καὶ διατριβή [prev. author] 5.38; πόλεμος οὐκ ἐνῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271e; μηδὲ μύλαν ἐνεῖμεν μηδὲ ὅλμον “IG” 22.[same place]}}; also, **to be mentioned in** a treaty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.43, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 974}}; χρόνος ἐνέσται time **will be necessary,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80}}; ἡ βὴξ ἔνι the cough **is persistent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.12.}}
**II** **to be possible,** ἄρνησις οὐκ ἔ. ὧν ἀνιστορεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 578; τῶνδ᾽ ἄρνησις οὐκ ἔ. μοι [prev. author] “El.” 527}}; τίς δ᾽ ἔνεστί μοι λόγο; what plea **is possible** for me [to make]? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 998; οὐκ ἐνῆν πρόφασις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.25; οὐκ ἐνέσται αὐτῷ λόγος οὐδὲ εἷς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.41; εἴ τι ἄλλο ἐνῆν [prev. author] 18.190; ἐνούσης οὐδεμιᾶς ἔτ᾽ ἀποστροφῆς [prev. author] 24.9.}}
**II.2** _impersonal_, with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **it is in one's power,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 296, “Ant.” 213, etc.}}: with _infinitive_ only, οὔκουν ἔ. καὶ μεταγνῶνα; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1270; οὐ γὰρ δὴ τοῦτό γ᾽ ἔνεστιν εἰπεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.14; πῶς ἔ. ἢ πῶς δυνατό; [prev. author] 57.24, etc.}}; οὐκ ἔνεστι **it is** not **possible,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 22.7}}; ὃ μὴ νεώς γε τῆς ἐμῆς ἔνι which **it is** not **possible** [to get] from my ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 648 (sed to be read ἔπι): ἔνι is frequently in this sense, ἃ δὲ ἔνι [λέγειν] 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.4; δι᾽ ὀργήν γ᾽ ἔνι φῆσαι πεποιηκέναι [prev. author] 21.41}}; ὡς ἔνι ἥδιστα in the pleasantest way **possible,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.5.9, compare 3.8.4; ὡς ἔνι μάλιστα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.4.14, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.465, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.” 6, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.218c}}: _imperfect_, ὡς ἐνῆν ἄριστα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tyr.” 17.}}
**II.2.b** ἔνεστιν ὑμᾶς εἰδέναι **it is relevant, pertinent**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 486.12 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.3** _participle_ ἐνόν, _absolutely_, ἐνὸν αὐτοῖς σώζεσθαι **since it was in** them, **was possible for** them, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.3.2, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 9.}}
**II.4** τὰ ἐνόντα **all things possible**: τὸ πλῆθος τῶν ἐ. εἰπεῖν the **possible materials** for a speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.110, compare 11.44; τῶν ἐ... ἐν τῷ πράγματι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 235b; τῶν φαινομένων καὶ ἐ. τὰ κράτιστα ἑλέσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.190}}; ἐκ τῶν ἐ. **as well as one can under the circumstances,** {{Refs|[prev. work]256}}; τὰ ἐ. καὶ τὰ ἁρμόττοντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1450b5: in _sing._, πᾶν τὸ ἐνὸν ἐκλέγων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.59.}}
**II.4.b** τὰ ἐνόντα **cargo** or **stores** in a ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 488c}}; **contents** of a basket, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 414.20 (2nd c.AD).}}

1763:
** ἐνῐ-αυτός**, ὁ,
(ἐνί, αὐτός) properly **anniversary,** μηδὲ τᾷ ὑστεραίᾳ μηδ᾽ ἐν ταῖς δεκάταις μηδ᾽ ἐν τοῖς ἐνιαυτοῖς {{Refs|“Michel” 995 C 49 (_plural_, Delph.): hence πρὸ τῶ ἐ.}} before the **lapse of a year,** {{Refs|Leges Gortynensium 9.29}}; ἐνιαυτῷ **on the expiry of a year,** {{Refs|[prev. work]1.35}}; and so, **any long period of time, cycle, period,** ἔτος ἦλθε περιπλομένων ἐνιαυτῶν as **times** rolled on the year came, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.16; ἐπιπλομένων ἐ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 493, “Sc.” 87; χρονίους ἐτῶν παλαιῶν ἐνιαυτούς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 347; πόλιν ἐνιαυτόν τινα ἔδοσαν ἐνοικεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68}}; ὁ μέγας ἐ., of a Pythagorean **cycle,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eudemus Philosophus cited in 2nd c.AD: Theon Smyrnaeus Philosophus p.198 H}}.; also of **the Metonic Cycle** of nineteen years, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.36}}; of a period of {{Refs 1st c.AD+|600 years, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.3.9:— ἀΐδιος ἐ. 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.4.2.}}
**2** ={ἔτος}, **a year,** εἴνατός ἐστι περιτροπέων ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.295; δεκάτους περιτελλομένους ἐ. 8.404; Διὸς ἐνιαυτοί 2.134}}; μῆνές τε καὶ ἐνιαυτῶν περίοδοι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 47a; ἐ. ἡμερῶν LXX.Lev.25.29; ἐνιαυτόν}}**during a year,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.288}}; αἱ σπονδαὶ ἐνιαυτὸν ἔσονται Indut. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118; ἐπεί κε ὠνίαυτος ἐξέλθῃ “IG” 12(2).1.12 (Mytil., 4th c.BC)}}; τὸν πρῶτον ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.8}}; ὁπηνίκα.. τοὐνιαυτοῦ at what time **in the year,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 569.7}}; δὶς τοῦ ἐ. twice **a year,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 118e; τοῦ ἐ.}}**every year,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.23; ἑκάστου ἐ. [prev. author] “Ath.” 3.4; but ἕκαστον τὸν ἐ. “IG” 2.1055.4: with Preps., δι᾽ ἐνιαυτοῦ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator “fragment” 28}}; δι᾽ ἐ. πέμπτου **every** five **years,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 119d}}; θητεύσαμεν εἰς ἐ. for **a year,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.444; τελεσφόρον εἰς ἐ. 19.32}}; κατ᾽ ἐνιαυτὸν ἄρξαι for **a year,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93}}; or, **every year,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.17, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 38.5; καθ᾽ ἕκαστον ἐ. [prev. author] 89}}; ἐπ᾽ ἐ. for **a year,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 945b, etc.}}; μετὰ τὸν ἐ. at the end of **the year,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138}}; παρ᾽ ἐνιαυτὸν ἄρχειν in alternate **years,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.65}}; πρὸ ἐνιαυτοῦ **a year** before, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.147e}}; ἐς τὸν σᾶτες ἐ. for the current **year,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.256 (from Phintias)}}; ἐν τῷ καθ᾽ ἕτος ἐ. in the current **year**, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3641b5 (from Lampsacus).}}
**3** Ἐνιαυτός, personified, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 19, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 127.3 (assuming variant), 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 3.41 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**II** name for a **Cornucopiae,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 2, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.783c.}}

1764:
** ἐνίστημι**,
causal in _present_, _future_ and _aorist 1_ _active_, and _aorist 1_ _middle_:— **put, place in,** ἵππον ἐν λίθοις ἐνιστάναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.16; στήλας ἐνίστη ἐς τὰς χώρας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.102}}; εἰς αὐτὴν (i.e. τὴν πόλιν) ἡνίοχον ἐνστῆσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 266e}}; τοὺς ἱπποκόμους εἰς (i.e. amongst) τοὺς ἱππέας ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 5.6: with _dative_, ἱστὸν ἐνεστήσαντο μεσόδμῃ 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.563.}}
**2** in Law, **institute** an heir, ἐ. κληρονόμους τοὺς υἱούς {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 151.75 (6th c.AD).}}
**3** _aorist 1_ _middle_, also, **begin,** ὅσαι τὸ πρᾶγμα τοῦτ᾽ ἐνεστήσαντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 268; οὐδὲν πώποτε τῶν πραγμάτων ἐνεστήσασθ᾽ ὀρθῶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.21}}; ὁ τοιοῦτον ἀγῶν᾽ ἐνστησάμενος {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.4}}; ἐ. τὸ πρᾶγμα, Latin _rem instituere,_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 951a28; ἀρχὰς τῆς γενέσεως 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.10.4}}; ὀργὴν καὶ μῖσος πρός τινα ἐνστήσασθαι **to begin to show**.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.82.9; πρᾶξιν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 16}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.53.}}
**4** ἐνστήσασθαι τὸ μέγεθος **determine** the size, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 50.29.}}
**B** _passive_, with _aorist_ 2, _perfect_, and _pluperfect_ _active_:—**to be set in, stand in,** λὁχοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 896; ἐν τῷ νηῷ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.91: _absolutely_, πύλαι ἐνεστᾶσι ἑκατόν [prev. author] 1.179, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 50d, etc.}}
**B.2** **enter upon, take possession of,** ὁ νικάσας ἐν τὰν οὐσίαν ἐνίσταται τὰν τοῦ ἁλόντος {{Refs|“Foed. Delph.Pell.” 2 B 14.}}
**B.II** **to be appointed,** σοῦ ἐνεστεῶτος βασιλέος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120, compare 6.59; ἐς ἀρχήν [prev. author] 3.68; ἐς τυραννίδας [prev. author] 2.147.}}
**B.III** **to be upon, threaten,** with _dative_ _person_, τοιούτων τοῖσι Σπαρτιήτῃσι ἐνεστεώτων πρηγμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.83; τὸν πόλεμον τὸν ἐνστάντα σοὶ καὶ τῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.2}}; in war, **press hard,** τινί {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.97.1}}: _absolutely_, **begin,** [τοῦ θέρους] ἐνισταμένου {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.8.2; ἐνισταμένου τοῦ ἐνιαυτοῦ LXX.1Ki.12.24}}; **to be at hand, arise,** ὁ τότ᾽ ἐνστὰς πόλεμος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.89, compare 139, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.71.4; τοῦ πολέμου πρὸς Φίλιππον ὑμῖν ἐνεστηκότος 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.58}}: especially in _perfect_ _participle_, **pending, present,** μιᾶς ἐνεστώσης δίκης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 779, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.45, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.14; ὁ νῦν ἐνεστηκὼς ἀγών 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 7; so οὐδενὸς ἡμῖν ἐνεστῶτος πρὸς αὐτούς “PStrassb.” 91.21 (1st c.BC)}}; of Time, **instant, present,** τοῦ ἐνεστῶτος μηνός Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.157}}; ἡ ἐνεστῶσα κακία, ἀνάγκη, {{NT+4th c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.60, NT.1Cor.7.26; κατὰ τὸν ἐ. καιρόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1366b23; ἀγαθὸν ἐνεστὼς ἢ μέλλον “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.94; compare ἐνεστάναι τὸν πάντα χρόνον ὡς τὸν ἐνιαυτὸν ἐνεστηκέναι λέγομεν 2nd c.BC: Apollodorus Seleuciensis Stoicus 3.260.}}
**B.III.2** esp. Grammars, ὁ ἐνεστὼς (i.e. χρόνος) the **present tense,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Stoic. 2.48, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 638.22, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 58.7, +others; also ἐνεστῶσα συντέλεια}}**the state of completion expressed by the perfect tense,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 205.15: also in}}_aorist_, τοῦ ποτὲ ἐνστάντος **when** the moment **has arrived,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.3.13}}; τὰ ἐνεστηκότα πράγματα **present** circumstances, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.6; so τὰ ἐνεστῶτα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.26.3.}}
**B.IV** **stand in the way, resist, block,** τοῖς ποιουμένοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.69; τῇ φυγῇ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 13; τῇ αὐξήσει [prev. author] “Rom.” 25; πρὸς πᾶσάν τινι πολιτείαν [prev. author] “Arist.” 3, compare “Marc.” 22}}: _absolutely_, **stand in the way,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.23; in argument, ἐνέστηκεν ὃ νυνδὴ Κέβης ἔλεγε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 77b}}; ὁ ἐνεστηκώς **the opponent** in a lawsuit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 45.28 (Halic., 5th c.BC).}}
**B.IV.2** in Logic, **object,** τῷ καθόλου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 157b3; πρὸς τὸν ἔξω λόγον [prev. author] “APo.” 76b26: _absolutely_, [prev. author] “Rh.” 1402b24, +others; ἐ. ὅτι.. [prev. author] “APr.” 69b6; ὡς.. [prev. author] “EN” 1172b35, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 176.23.}}
**B.IV.3** of the Roman tribunes, **exercise the right of intercessio, veto,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.16.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 10, +others}}
**B.V** of fluids, **congeal, freeze,** ὕδωρ ἐνεστηκός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.13.1}}; **become impacted in,** ἐνιστάμενον ἐπὶ τὰ τοῦ στομάχου στενά (i.e. γάλα) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Alex.” 26.}}

1765:
** ἐνισχύω**,
**strengthen, confirm,** ὁ χρόνος ταῦτα -ύσει πάντα {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “νόμος” 3; ἄγγελος ἐνισχύων αὐτόν NT.Luke.22.43}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Gal.fragment” 7.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **prevail in** or **among,** ἐν ταῖς πόλεσι ἐνισχύει τὰ νόμιμα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1180b4: _absolutely_, [prev. author] “PA” 653a31 +others; τοῦτ᾽ ἐνισχύειν ἑκάστῳ 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 63, compare 67; παρά τισιν ἐ. ἐν παροιμίας μέρει 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.58}}; ἐνίσχυσεν ὡς.. **the opinion prevailed** that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.57.}}

1767:
** ἐννέᾰ**,
_Doric dialect_ also **ἐννῆ** (which see), _indeclinable_, **nine,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.174, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.258, etc.; Μοῦσαι ἐννέα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 917, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.60}}; τρὶς ἐννέα κλῶνας, in a religious ceremony, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 483}}; τρὶς ἐ. ἔτη Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26.}}
**2** as a round number for, **many,** τρὶς ἐννέα φῶτας ἔπεφνεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.785, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.577, Scholia 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 781}}. (Cf. Latin _novem,_ Sanskrit _náva,_ etc.)

1769:
** ἐνεός**
(in codices sometimes ἐννεός {{NT|NT.Act.9.7, etc.)}}, ά, όν, **dumb, speechless,** frequently joined with κωφός, as {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 206d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 536b4, “Pr.” 961b14, “Sens.” 437a16; without κωφός, ἐνεοῖς ἀνθρώποις ὁμοίους 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 356, cf. LXX.Isa.56.10, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 8, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 62.16: _accusative_ to 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, ὃς οὔτε ἀκούει οὔτε λαλεῖ}} **deaf and dumb,** as in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.33.}} _adverb_ -εῶς uncertain reading in Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 6.53.}}
**2** **senseless, stupid,** ἀπείρους καὶ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.140d.}}
**3** of things, **useless,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 8; ἐς τὸ ἐ. κεῖσθαι [prev. passage]}}
**4** **dumbfounded, astonished,** εἱστήκεισαν ἐ. {{NT|NT.Act.[same place]}}

1771:
** ἔννοια**, ἡ,
(νοῦς) **act of thinking, reflection, cogitation** (συντονία διανοίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 414a); ἄξιον ἐννοίας [prev. author] “Lg.” 657a, +others}}
**2** **notion, conception,** χρόνου ἔννοια {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 47a; ἐν ταῖς περὶ τὸ ὂν.. ἐννοίαις [prev. author] “Phlb.” 59d}}; ἔ. λαβεῖν to form **an idea,** opposed to αἴσθησιν λαβεῖν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 73c; τοῦ καλοῦ ἔ. ἔχειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1179b15; ἐννοίας χάριν λέγειν [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1073b12}}; ἔννοιαι, opposed to φαντασίαι, αἰσθήσεις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “MA” 701b17; κατὰ ἀθρόαν ἔ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.23U. (but κατὰ πᾶσαν ἔ. θυμοῦ}} every **kind, variety** of anger, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.90 W.); δοξαστικαὶ ἔ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 24; εἰς ἔ. ἔρχεσθαί τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.57.4; εἰς ἔ. τινὸς ἄγειν τινά [prev. work] 49.10}}; ἡ κοινὴ ἔ. the common **notion,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 10.27.8}}; κοιναὶ ἔ. **axioms,** heading in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra}}; general **ideas,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.154, etc.}}; ψιλὴ ἔ. mere, i.e. vague, **notion,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 18.1.}}
**3** **intent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1026}}; ἔννοιαν λαβεῖν form **a design,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 1027}}; **intention** of a testator, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.13; ἔ. ἔχειν περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 769e}}; ἔ. ἐμποιεῖν put an **idea** into one's head, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.150; ἔ. ἐμπίπτει τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.13.}}
**4** **good sense, better judgement,** παρὰ τὴν ἔννοιαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1077d.}}
**II** **sense** of a word, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 69.21.}}
**III** Rhet., **thought,** opposed to **diction** (λέξις), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.4, compare “Prog.” 6.}}

1772:
** ἔννομος**, ον,
**ordained by law, lawful, legal,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.84}}; [χθονὸς αἶσα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “P.” 9.57; δίκα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 384 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1651, etc.}}; ἔννομα πείσονται they will suffer **lawful punishment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.67}}; ἔ. ὁμολογία, πολιτεία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 921c, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.5; σὺμ ψάφοις ταῖς ἐ. “Supp.epigram” 2.277 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; ἐκκλησία {{NT|“IG” 9(1).3 (_Locrian dialect_), NT.Act.19.39}}; ἡλικία, χρόνοι, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“POxy.” 247.12 (1st c.AD), “Michel” 468.29 (2nd c.BC).}} _adverb_ -μως, ζημιοῦσθαι, διοικεῖσθαι,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.12, 30.35, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 56.7}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερον**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1204.24 (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** of persons, **keeping within the law, upright, just,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 404 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 424e}}; also, **subject to the law,** μὴ ὢν ἄνομος Θεοῦ, ἀλλ᾽ ἔ. Χριστοῦ {{NT|NT.1Cor.9.21.}}
**II** (νέμομαι) **feeding in,** i.e. **inhabiting,** οἳ γᾶς τότ᾽ ἦσαν ἔννομοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 565 (Lyric poetry).}}

1773:
** ἔννῠχ-ος**, ον,
= {ἐννύχιος}, ἄγγελος ἦλθε.. ἔννυχος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.716, compare 3rd c.BC: Maiistas Epicus 16; ἔ. κοῖται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.25; ὄψεις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 645}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, ἔννυχα λίαν ἀναστάς {{NT|NT.Mark.1.35}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 110.}}
**II** epithet of Hades, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 501 (Lyric poetry).}}

1774:
** ἐνοικ-έω**,
**dwell in,** with _dative_ loci, Θήβαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1282, etc.; χώρα καλὴ ὥστε ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.25; κατὰ στέγην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1051; ἐνταῦθα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 95: _absolutely_, οὔ τι γὰρ κεκτήμεθ᾽.. αὐτό (i.e. τὸ σῶμα), πλὴν ἐνοικῆσαι βίον}}.. we possess it not, save **to dwell in** during life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 535, compare “Leg.Gort.” 4.34, “IG” 12(5).568,1100 (Ceos, 5th c.BC); [Θυρέαν] ἔδοσαν ἐνοικεῖν}}**dwell in,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.56, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.178.}}
**2** **to be present at,** συνελεύσει {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 3.10 (6th c.AD).}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **dwell upon, 'be at home in',** ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 316a6; τοῖς συγγράμμασιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 45.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **inhabit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.18, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1533, etc.: _absolutely_, οἱ ἐνοικοῦντες}}**the inhabitants,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.66, compare 1.4, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1330b8.}}

1775:
** ἑνότης**, ητος, ἡ,
(εἷς) **unity,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1018a7, “Ph.” 222a19, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.6.16, etc.; ἡ ἑ. ἐν ἑτερότητι 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 36; τοῦ αἵματος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 667b30.}}
**II** **union,** συμπάθεια πρὸς ἀλλήλους καὶ ἑ. ἰδιότροπος {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.13U.; τοῦ πνεύματος τῆς πίστεως NT.Eph.4.3, 13; ἑνότητα ποιεῖν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.769f.}}
**III** in concrete sense, ἀπογεγεννημένη ἑ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Nat.Herc.” 1634.1; τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἑ. 2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus 1055.7 F., compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 80 (_plural_).}}

1776:
** ἐνοχλ-έω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ and _poetry_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἐννοχλεῖς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.36}}: _imperfect_ with double augment ἠνώχλουν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.56, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.53, etc.}}: _future_ ἐνοχλήσω {{Refs|[prev. author] 15.153}}: _aorist_ ἠνώχλησα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.206}}: _perfect_ ἠνώχληκα {{Refs|[prev. author] 21.4}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ηθήσομαι**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.3, Polystr. p.8 W.}}; also -ήσομαι (in _passive_ sense) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] p.6 W., 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.36, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 11.19 (as variant)}}: _aorist_ _participle_ ἐνοχληθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 510:}}_perfect_ ἠνώχλημαι (παρ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.50}}:—**trouble, annoy,** τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.104d, 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 2.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.2, etc.}}; simply, **address,** {{Refs|PMag.Leid.W. 3.34}}:—_passive_, **to be troubled** or **annoyed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.34, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.20; ἡ ἐκκλησία ἠνωχλεῖτο 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.43}}; **to be unwell,** {{LXX|LXX.Gen.48.1, +others}}; of a horse, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.73 (3rd c.BC)}}; **to be overburdened with work,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PHamb. 27.18 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**2** with _dative_, **give trouble** or **annoyance to,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.21; τοῖς ἀκούουσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.7; τῇ ὑμετέρᾳ εὐδαιμονίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.13, compare 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 15, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 11.10; ἠνώχλει ἡμῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.5, etc.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **to be a trouble, a nuisance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 708, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.61U., etc.: with _neuter_}} _adjective_, ὅσα.. ἠνώχλησεν all **the trouble** he **has given,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.15}}: with _participle_, τὸ δὲ μὴ οὐκ ἠνώχλει λέγων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.56.}}
**II** **worry about, fuss over,** τὰς ἀρετὰς τὰς ὑπὸ τούτων ἐνοχλουμένας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 25.}} —Prose word, sometimes used in Comedy texts, never in Trag.

1777:
** ἔνοχος**, ον,
={ἐνεχόμενος}, **held in, bound by,** τοιαύταις δόξαις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1009b17; ταῖς εἰρημέναις βλάβαις [prev. author] “Pol.” 1337b17}}; [ἔθεσι γεροντικοῖς] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Apollodorus Comicus 7.2.}}
**2** with _genitive_, **connected with,** κοιλίης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 23.}}
**II** as law-term, **liable to, subject to,** νόμοις, δίκαις, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 869b; τῇ γραφῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.64; τῇ κρίσει NT.Matt.5.22; τῷ ὅρκῳ “PRyl.” 82.14 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; τοῖς ἐπιτιμίοις τοῦ φόνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.6; ζημίαις 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.9; ταῖς ἀραῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.201; δεσμῷ [prev. author] 51.4; ὅρκῳ “PHib.” 1.65.22 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; ἔ. ἀνοίαις **liable to the imputation of** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.7; ἁμαρτήμασι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.146; τοῖς αἰσχίστοις ἐπιτηδεύμασιν [prev. author] 1.185.}}
**II.2** ἔνοχος ψευδομαρτυρίοις **liable to action for..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 148b: with _genitive_, ἔ. τοῦ φόνου 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.46}}; βιαίων, λιποταξίου (i.e. δίκῃ, γραφῇ), {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 914e, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.5; ἱεροσυλίας LXX.2Mac.13.6; μοιχείας Vett. Val. 117.10}}; ἔ. θανάτου **liable to the penalty of** death, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 27.4, NT.Matt.26.66 (but θανάτῳ Wilcken “Chr.” 13.11 (1st c.AD))}}: with _infinitive_, ἔ. ἔστω ἀποτῖσαι {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2832.8 (from Aphrodisias).}}
**II.3** less frequently with Preps., ἔ. ἐν τοῖς αὐτοῖς Decrees cited in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.79; περὶ ταὐτά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1384b2; ἔνοχοι ἔντω ἐνς Ἀθαναίαν “IG” 4.554 (Argos, 6th-5th c.BC).}}
**II.4** **guilty, liable to the penalty for,** ἔ. τῷ φόνῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.11, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1269a3, compare “Rh.” 1380a3: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.1, 6.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 261a, etc.}}
**II.4.b** of property, **subject to liability,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PMasp. 312.86 (6th c.AD).}}

1781:
** ἐντέλλω**,
**enjoin, command,** _active_ only in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 269}}:— mostly in _middle_, τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.47, etc.}}; in a will, φίλοις ταῦτα ἐντέλλομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 66}}: with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.53, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 393e, etc.}}; ἐντείλασθαι ἀπὸ γλώσσης **command** by word of mouth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123}}: so in _perfect_, ἐντέταλται {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.1Ki.13.17; ἐντεταλμένοι εἴησαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.2.1, compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.9.9}}:—_passive_, τὰ ἐντεταλμένα **commands,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60, 5.73, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 462, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.3.}}
**II** **invest with legal powers, authorize to act,** ἐ. σοι καὶ ἐπιτρέπω {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLips.” 38.5 (4th c.AD),compare “PMasp.” 124.6 (6th c.AD).}}

1782:
** ἐντεῦθεν**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἐνθεῦτεν**, _adverb_, (related to ἔνθεν, as ἐνταῦθα to ἔνθα):
**I** of Place, **hence** or **thence,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.568, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 836, “Pers.” 488, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.42, etc.; τὸ γένος ἐ. ποθεν ἐκ Χίου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 271c}}; τἀντεῦθεν **matters there,** i.e. in the house, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1339; ἐ. καὶ ἐ. LXX.Num.22.24, NT.John.19.18}}; ἐ. κἀκεῖθεν{{Refs|Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.29H.; ἐντεῦθεν εἰς τυχόν}}**'go to Jericho',** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 184.}}
**II** of Time, **henceforth, thereupon,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 728, “Ph.” 384 (Lyric poetry, uncertain reading), etc.; also τὸ ἐ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, 27, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 198b}}; _Attic dialect_ also τοὐντεῦθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 792, +others; τἀντεῦθεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 60; τὸ ἐ. ἐπὶ τούτοις 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 8.17.}}
**III** causal, **thence, from that source,** τὸν βίον ἐ. ἐποιοῦντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5; ἐ. αἱ μάχαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1131a23; ἐ. ποθεν [prev. author] “Pol.” 1286b15}}; **therefore, in consequence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 949, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 399c.}}—_Attic dialect_ strengthened **ἐντευθενί** [ῑ], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 10, “Lys.” 92, etc.}}; compare ἐνμεντευθενί.

1783:
** ἔντευξις**, εως, ἡ,
(ἐντυγχάνω) **lighting upon, meeting with,** with _dative_, αἱ τοῖς λῃσταῖς ἐντεύξεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 298d.}}
**2** **converse, intercourse,** πρὸς τοὺς πολλούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1355a29: with _genitive_, “Vita Philonidis Epicurei” p.7 C}}.; ἐντεύξεις ποιεῖσθαί τισι hold **converse with**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.20; [ἡ πραγματεία] χρήσιμος πρὸς τὰς ἐ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 101a27, compare “Metaphorical” 1009a17, etc.; τὴν ἡλικίαν τῇ ἐντεύξει γνωρίζομεν 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.8.}}
**2.b** **manners, behaviour,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.47, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 5.1, 20.1.}}
**2.c** especially **sexual intercourse,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 51, “fragment” 61.}}
**3** ἐντεύξεις ὀχλικαί **speeches** to the mob, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 50.}}
**4** **petition**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.383.6 (3rd c.BC), “PFlor.” 55.18 (1st c.AD), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 11, etc.}}; **intercession** for a person, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.55, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “fragment” 130.7 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, NT.1Tim.2.1(_plural_).}}
**5** **reading, study,** ἡ ἔ. τῆς πραγματείας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.1.4, etc.}}

1784:
** ἔντῑμ-ος**, ον,
(τιμή),
**1** of persons, **in honour, honoured,** opposed to ἄτιμος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 281c, etc.}}; τινί by another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 239 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 25, etc.; παρά τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 21e}}: with _dative_ _of things_, **honoured with,** σπονδαῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1688 (_anapaest meter_)}}; **in office,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 564d}}; of **men of high rank** in Persia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.8, +others}}; opposed to ἄδοξοι, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.29; ={ἐπίτιμος}, Decrees cited in [same work] 59.104.}}]
**2** of things, τὰ θεῶν ἔ. **what is honoured in** their sight, their **ordinances** or **attributes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 77}}; ἔ. ποιῆσαι τὴν τέχνην hold it **in honour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.159; ἔ. ποιεῖν τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1286b15}}; ἔργα - ότερα (opposed to ἀναγκαιότερα){{Refs|[prev. work] 1255b28; δαπανήματα -ότατα [prev. author] “EN” 1122b35}}; χώρα ἔ. place **of honour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 985e}}; ἔ. ἀπόλυσις, = Latin _honesta missio,_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PHamb. 1.31.19 (2nd c.AD).}}
**3** _adverb_ -μως, ἄγειν τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528c, compare 1st c.BC(?): Satyrus Epigrammaticus “Vit.Eur.fragment” 39xviii27 (_passive_)}}; ἔχειν τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528b}}; also ἐ. ἔχειν to be **in honour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.7}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 63.17}}; -μως ἀπολελυμένος, = Latin _missus honesta missione,_ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|POxy. 1471.6 (1st c.AD), +others}}
**II** **doing honour, honourable** (to a person), λόγος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 855a.}}
**III** **valuable, highly valued,** [χώρα] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 352a12 (_comparative_),compare “PLond.” 5.1708.33 (6th c.AD)}}; of currency, **accepted** in exchange, opposed to ἀδόκιμον, νόμισμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 742a.}}

1786:
** ἐντόπ-ιος**, ον,
**local,** θεοὶ ἐ., ={ἐγχώριοι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 262d}}; νόμισμα, πλοιάρια, {{Refs|“Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 49,36}}; πόλεμοι ἐ. **civil** wars, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.83; ἡ ἐ. ἱστορία 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.35}}; ἐντόπιοι **local residents,** opposed to ξένοι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 5(2).491 (Megalopolis, 2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; opposed to Ἀλεξανδρεῖς, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.192.94 (1st c.AD).}}
**2** in Medicine texts, **local,** βάρος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.40.}}

1787:
** ἐντός**,
(ἐν) **within, inside,** opposed to ἐκτός:
**I** _preposition_ with _genitive_, which mostly follows, but may precede, τείχεος ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.380, +others, compare Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. 1920.33 (_Boeotian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}; ἐ. Ὀλύμπου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 37; στέρνων ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 77 (_anapaest meter_); σ᾽ ἔθρεψεν ἐ... ζώνης [prev. author] “Eu.” 607}}; ἐ. ἐμεωυτοῦ **in** my **senses, under** my own **control,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.47; ἐ. ἑωυτοῦ γίνεσθαι [prev. author] 1.119, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.1; ἐ. ὢν εἰπεῖν αὑτοῦ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.20; ἐ. τῶν λογισμῶν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 32}}; ἐ. ὑμῶν **in** your **hearts,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.Luke.17.21; τῶν μαθημάτων ἐ. 4th c.BC: Dicaearchus Geographus 1.30; γραμμάτων ἐ. 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.3; ἐ. εἶναι τῶν συμβαινόντων παθῶν}}**acquainted with,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.120}}; ἐ. τοξεύματος **within** shot, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 991, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.23}}; οὐδ᾽ ἐντὸς πολλοῦ πλησιάζειν not **within** a great distance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 195b, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.77}}; ἐ. ποιεῖν put **within,** τῶν τειχῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.5; ἐ. ποιεῖσθαι τῶν ἐπιτάκτων [prev. author] 6.67; ἐ. πλαισίου ποιησάμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.16}}; of troops, ἐ. αὐτῶν **within** their own **lines,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.10.3: also with Verbs of motion, τείχεος ἐ. ἰόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.374; πύργων ἔπεμψεν ἐντός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 12.}}
**I.2** **within,** i.e. **on this side,** ἐ. Ἅλυος ποταμοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6, compare 8.47, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.16}}; ἡ ἐ. Ἱσπανία, = Latin _Hispania Citerior,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 10; ἐ. τοῦ Πόντου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.46; ἐ. ὅρων Ἡρακλείων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 25c}}; ἐ. τῶν μέτρων τετμημένον μέταλλον **within** the bounds of the adjacent property, an encroachment, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 35; τῶν μέτρων ἐ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.36}}; also ἐ. τῶν πρῳρέων.. καὶ τοῦ αἰγιαλοῦ **between**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.100.}}
**I.3** of Time, **within,** ἐ. οὐ πολλοῦ χρόνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.69; ἐ. εἴκοσιν ἡμερῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.39, compare “IG” 12.114.40, etc.; ἐ. ἑξήκοντ᾽ ἐτῶν 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 20.2}}; ἐ. ἑσπέρας **short of,** i.e. **before,** evening, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.11}}; ἐ. ἑβδόμης **before** the seventh of the month, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; οἱ τῆς ἡλικίας ἐ. γεγονότες **short of** manhood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.50}}; τῆς πρεπούσης ἐ. ἡλικίας **within** the fitting limits of age, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 18d.}}
**I.4** with Numbers, ἐ. εἴκοσιν [ἐτῶν] **under** twenty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 984}}; ἐ. δραχμῶν πεντήκοντα **within,** i.e. **under**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 953b.}}
**I.5** of Degrees of relationship, ἐ. ἀνεψιότητος **within** the relationship of cousins, **nearer than** cousins, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 871b, Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.57.}}
**II** _adverb_ **within,** ἐ. ἐέργειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.845, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.88; χώρην ἐ. ἀπέργειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.116; ἐ. ἔχειν τινάς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.78}}; ἐ. ποιῆσαι or ποιήσασθαι, [{{Refs|[prev. author] 5.2, 6.75: frequently with the _Article_, ἐκ τοῦ ἐ., ={ἔντοσθε}, [prev. author] 2.76}}]; τὰ ἐ. the **inner parts** of the body (of ἥ τε φάρυγξ καὶ ἡ γλῶσσα),{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 49, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 334c, etc.}}; τοὐντός, opposed to τοὔξω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 302; ἐ.}}**in the Mediterranean,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 393a12.}}

1788:
** ἐντρέπ-ω**,
**turn about,** τὰ νῶτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.211}}; ἐξεστραμμένην ἕδραν ἐ. **reduce** prolapsed anus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.365}}; of a muscle, **turn** the eye **in,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “UP” 10.9 (_passive_).}}
**2** mostly _metaphorically_, **make** one **turn, put** him **to shame,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.1Cor.4.14, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.17, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 3.135, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.29.}}
**3** **alter,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 15; τὴν φωνὴν εἰς μέλος [prev. author] “Pseudol.” 7.}}
**4** _middle_, ἐντρέψασθαι· τὸ εἴσω τρέψαι τὸ ἱμάτιον, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** _middle_ or _passive_, _future_ ἐντραπήσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Lev.26.41, +others}}; **turn about, hesitate,** especially **feel misgiving** or **compunction,** στείχωμεν ἤδη μηδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐντρεπώμεθα (where {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia compares ἐντροπαλίζομενος) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1541; ἐνετρέποντο.. ἐν ἑαυτοῖς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.2.6 (probably conjecture).}}
**II.2** with _genitive_ _person_, **turn towards, give heed** or **regard to, respect, reverence,** οὐδέ νυ σοί περ ἐντρέπεται φίλον ἦτορ ἀνεψιοῦ κταμένοι; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.554, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.60; συμμάχου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 90; δωμάτων [prev. author] “OT” 1226; νόμων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 52c, etc.; ὧν ἐντρέπου σὺ μηδέν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 724}}: with _infinitive_, **take heed to**.., φεύγειν ὀλεσήνορας ὅρκους ἐντρέπευ conjecture in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 400}}: _aorist 2_ _passive_, [οὐκ] ἐντρεπέντος τοῦ Ἀμώσιος since {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} **paid** no **attention**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 5.24 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.3** later with _accusative_, **reverence, feel regard for,** τὴν πολιάν {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 71, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.10.3, +others, NT.Mark.12.6.}}
**II.3.b** **feel shame on account of,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.49.7.}}
**II.4** _absolutely_, **feel shame** or **fear**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 62.29 (2nd c.BC), NT.2Thes.3.14, NT.Tit.2.8.}}

1791:
** ἐντροπ-ή**, ἡ,
**turning towards**: only _metaphorically_ {{Refs|compare (ἐντρέπω 11.2), ἐντροπήν τινος ἔχειν}} **respect for** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 299, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.52.2, “OGI” 323.7 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC)}}, etc.: _absolutely_, **modesty,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 5, NT.1Cor.6.5, etc.; ἐ. καὶ αἰδώς 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 2.10.}}
**2** **humiliation,** {{LXX|LXX.Psa.34(35).26, +others}}

1792:
** ἐντρῠφ-άω**,
**revel in, delight in,** with _dative_, ἐξουσία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 362.24; γαμηλίῳ λέχει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 535.8; ἡδοναῖς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.71, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 21}}; in good sense, δικαιοσύνῃ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.258}}; of persons, Πελοπίδᾳ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 30; ἔν τινι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 65.20}}; in bad sense, ἐν ταῖς ἀγάπαις {{NT|NT.2Pet.2.13}}; κόμαι ἀνέμοις ἐνετρύφων it was **playing in** the wind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Chaeremon Tragicus 1.7: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.30, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.666.}}
**II** **treat haughtily** or **contemptuously,** τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 588, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 18, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.35}}; **exult over,** τινὸς συμφοραῖς {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator}}**ad Ath.**279c; ἔν τινι {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Hab.1.10: _absolutely_, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 23}}:—_passive_, **to be made a mock of,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lys.” 6, “Caes.” 64.}}
**III** **use** or **abuse at pleasure,** τοῖς νόμοις, τοῖς συνοῦσι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Abd.” 10, “Merc.Cond.” 35, +others}}

1793:
** ἐντυγχάνω**,
_future_ -τεύξομαι: _aorist 2_ ἐνέτῠχον: _perfect_ ἐντετύχηκα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.395, also ἐντέτευχα “Klio” 15.35 (Delph., 1st c.BC)}}: _aorist 1_ _passive_ ἐνετεύχθην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.170, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 9}}:—**light upon, fall in with, meet with,** with _dative_ _person_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.134, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 689, etc.}}; ὀλίγοι τινὲς ὧν ἐντετύχηκα (i.e. τούτων οἷς..) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 531e, compare “Grg.” 509a, “Prt.” 361e; κατ᾽ ὄψιν ἐ. τινί 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 1.}}
**2** with _dative_ _of things_, κακοῖς ἐ., ={τυγχάνω ὢν ἐν κακοῖς}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 433}}; οὑντυγχάνων (i.e. τοῖς πράγμασιν) conjecture Valck. in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 287}}; ἐ. τῷ νώτῳ, of the crocodile, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.70}}; ὁ ἐ. τοῖς.. τοξεύμασι **he who fell in** their **way,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.40}}; of obstacles, ἐ. τάφροις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.10; λόφῳ [prev. work] 4.2.10.}}
**3** _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1032, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 848, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 24.8; οἱ ἐντυχόντες}}**chance persons,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.132; οἱ ἐντυγχάνοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.36; τὴν ὠμότητα, ᾗ καθ᾽ ἁπάντων χρῆται τῶν ἐντυγχανόντων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.88, compare 183: _sing._, ὁ ἐντυχών 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.61, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.144b.}}
**3.b** ἐν δὲ μηνὸς πρῶτον τύχεν ἆμαρ it **chanced to be..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Pae.” 2.75.}}
**4** **obtain an audience** or **interview,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 88.8, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 1.3}}:—_passive_, **to be appealed to, consulted,** περί τινων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.170.}}
**5** of thunder, **strike,** κεραυνὸς οἷς ἂν ἐντύχῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.14}}; but hardly so in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1329, παῦλαν ἴσθι.. μήποτ᾽ ἐντυχεῖν νόσου (ἂν τυχεῖν Pors.).}}
**6** rarely with _genitive_, λελυμένης τῆς γεφύρης ἐντυχόντες **having found** the bridge broken up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.140}}; τῶν παρ᾽ ἡμῖν ἐντυχὼν Ἀσκληπιδῶν **having falling in with** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1333.}}
**II** **converse with, talk to,** τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 41b, “Phd.” 61c, etc.; οὐκ ἄχαρις ἐντυχεῖν [prev. author] “Ep.” 360c; οὐκ ἀηδὴς ἐ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 112.}}
**II.2** **have sexual intercourse with,** τινί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 20.}}
**II.3** **petition, appeal to,** τινὶ περί τινος (_masculine_) {{NT+2nd c.BC+|NT.Act.25.24; τῷ βασιλεῖ περὶ τούτων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.76.9}}; ὁ ἐντυγχάνων the **petitioner**, {{Refs|“OGI” 669.5}}; ἐ. κατά τινος **plead** against, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“PGiss.” 1.36.15 (2nd c.BC), LXX.1Mac.8.32, NT.Rom.11.2; τῷ βασιλεῖ τὴν ἀπόλυσιν LXX.3Mac.6.37; τῷ διοικητῇ “PTeb.” 58.43 (2nd c.BC)}}: with _infinitive_, **entreat** one **to** do, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus “fragment” 47 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 55; ἐ. ὅπως.. [prev. author] “Ages.” 25}}:—_passive_, ὑπὲρ φυγάδων ἐντευχθείς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cat.Ma.” 9.}}
**III** of books, **meet with,** βιβλίῳ ἀνδρὸς σοφοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 177b, compare “Ly.” 214b; οἱ ἐντετυχηκότες ταῖς ἱερωτάταις βίβλοις 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.395}}; hence, **read,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 27, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 12, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.210d, etc.; οἱ ἐντυγχάνοντες}}**readers,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.3.10, 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 1.1; ἐντυχὼν ὑμῶν τῷ ψηφίσματι “IG” 12(3).176 (Epist. Hadriani), compare 5(1).1361.7 (Epist. Commodi).}}

1795:
** ἐντῠπ-όω**,
**carve** or **mould in** or **upon,** τῷ νομίσματι ἐνετύπωσεν ἀπήνην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 568; ἐς τὰ νομίσματα ξιφίδια δύο 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 47.25; ἄγαλμα 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.8.6}}; also of a painter, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“APl.” 4.282 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus): _metaphorically_, σχῆμα τῇ ψυχῇ ἐντετύπωκεν ὁ θεός 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.106}}:—_middle_, Φειδίαν ἐν μέσῃ τῇ ἀσπίδι τὸ ἑαυτοῦ πρόσωπον ἐντυπώσασθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 399b35}}:—_passive_,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Aristeas 67; τύλοι ἐντετυπωμένοι 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.43}}; **to be imprinted,** of a birth-mark, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.81c}}; also, **to be flattened** by pressure, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 4.7, “Hippiatrica” 38: _metaphorically_, ἐντετύπωται ταῖς θύραις}} **is like a piece of carving on** the doors, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.7.11.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, τὸ ἰδίωμα τῇ λέξει ἐ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 10.6.}}

1796:
** ἐνυβρ-ίζω**,
**insult** or **mock** one **in** a thing, τινά τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 342; τινὰ ἐν κακοῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 68; μήμου ἐνυβρίξῃς ἁγνὸν τάφον “epigram Gr.” 195 (from Vaxos).}}
**2** with _dative_ _person_, **insult,** γυναιξίν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.26.3, compare “POxy.” 237 vi 17 (2nd c.AD)}}; εἴς τινα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 34.2.}}
**3** _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 720.}}
**4** in _passive_, in Medicine texts, of ulcers, **to be irritated,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.120.}}

1798:
** ἐνύπν-ιον**, τό,
(ὕπνος) **thing seen in sleep,** in _apposition_ with ὄνειρος, θεῖός μοι ἐνύπνιον ἦλθεν ὄνειρος a dream from the gods, **a vision in sleep,** came to me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.495, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.56; ἐ. τὰ ἐς ἀνθρώπους πεπλανημένα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16}}.; ἐ. παιδός **the vision** of a boy, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.125 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus): used adverbially, ἐ. ἑστιᾶσθαι}} 'to feast with the Barmecide', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1218; κακοδαίμον᾽ οὕτω δεσπότην οὐδ᾽ ἐ. ἰδών 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 169; later κατ᾽ ἐνύπνιον “Anthologia Graeca” 11.150 (2nd c.AD: Ammianus Epigrammaticus)}}; compare{ἐνύπνιος}
**2** after [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ={ὄνειρος}}}], **dream,** ὄψις ἐνυπνίου the vision **of a dream,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.54; ὄψις ἐμφανὴς ἐνυπνίων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 518, compare 226, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 572b; ἐνυπνίῳ πιθέσθαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.79; ἐ. ἰδεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 290b; τέλεον τὸ ἐ. ἀποτετέλεσται [prev. author] “R.” 443b; ἐνύπνια κρίνειν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.29, “Sammelb.” 685 (2nd c.BC):— 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus (1.1 b) distinguishes ἐνύπνιον}} **a mere dream,** and ὄνειρος **a significant, prophetic one;** but the distinction is not generally observed, except by Philo.

1799:
** ἐνώπ-ιος**, ον,
(ὤψ) **facing, to the front,** πρό τ᾽ ἐνώπια {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 4.17; ἐνώπιος ἐνωπίῳ λαλεῖν}}**face to face,** {{LXX|LXX.Exo.33.11}}; ἄρτοι ἐ. **shew**bread, {{Refs|[prev. work] 25.29(30)}}; διαστολῶν γεγονυιῶν ὑμῖν καὶ ἐνοπίοις (sic) καὶ διὰ γραμμάτων **in person**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 110.36 (2nd c.BC),compare “Sammelb.” 3925.6 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II** _neuter_ ἐνώπιον as _adverb_, **face to face,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.152}}; **in person,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(5).1061.10 (from Carthaea), “PTeb.” 14.13 (2nd c.BC)}}: as _preposition_ with _genitive_, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.43 codices, “PCair.Zen.” 73.14 (3rd c.BC), “PGrenf.” 1.38.11 (2nd-1st c.BC), NT.Rom.12.17, NT.Gal.1.20, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 1.1; ἐ. θεῶν “SIG” 2843.7 (Delph., 2nd c.AD)}}. Regul. _adverb_ **-ίως**{{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
